// Interview problems: advanced (conceptual/symbolic), Hibbeler. Fields: id, title, q (statement), figs, figDesc, sol (examiner-only reference), answers.
(window.EXAMPLES_ADV=window.EXAMPLES_ADV||{}).mom=[{"id":"1.1","title":"When is axial stress uniform over a section?","q":"A prismatic bar of cross-sectional area A (centroid C) carries a resultant axial force P through a section far from the ends. (a) Show that the assumption of uniform normal stress σ = P/A over the section is only consistent with equilibrium if P acts through the centroid; find the location of the line of action of P if the stress varies linearly across the section instead, σ = a + b·y (y measured from the centroid, b ≠ 0). (b) State Saint-Venant\u0027s principle and explain why it justifies using σ = P/A at a section a distance of about one bar width from a point load, but not at the load itself.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Equilibrium of the cut segment: $P=\\int\\sigma\\,dA$ and moment about the centroidal axis $M=\\int\\sigma y\\,dA$ (resultant P at offset e gives $M=Pe$).\n2. Uniform σ: $\\int\\sigma y\\,dA=\\sigma\\int y\\,dA=0$ because y is measured from the centroid, so e = 0. Conversely a uniform field can only be statically equivalent to a force through C.\n3. Linear σ = a + b y: $P=aA+b\\int y\\,dA=aA$, so a = P/A; $M=b\\int y^2dA=bI$, so $e=M/P=bI/(aA)=b r^2/a$ with $r^2=I/A$. Pure uniform (b=0) gives e=0, and e grows with the gradient b (this is the eccentric-load/bending superposition of Ch. 8).\n4. Saint-Venant: statically equivalent load systems produce the same stresses except locally, at distances of the order of the largest cross-section dimension from where the load is applied. Near a point load the stress is highly non-uniform (theoretically unbounded under a concentrated force), but it smooths to P/A within about one width.\nProbe: what happens to average stress if the bar is very short? (principle fails, no uniform region). Why does the Saint-Venant region matter for the bolt-hole and grip regions of test specimens?","answers":["Uniform σ requires P through the centroid (∫σy dA = 0 ⇒ e = 0)","For σ = a + b y: a = P/A, M = bI, so e = bI/(aA) = b r²/a (r² = I/A)","Saint-Venant: statically equivalent loadings give the same stress beyond roughly one characteristic cross-section dimension from the load"],"src":"Hibbeler §1.4 Average Normal Stress in an Axially Loaded Bar; §4.1 Saint-Venant\u0027s Principle"},{"id":"1.2","title":"Pin in double shear: which failure mode governs?","q":"A solid circular pin of diameter d joins a clevis (two outer plates) to a central bar of thickness t. The pin carries a force P transmitted through the central bar, so it is in double shear at the two clevis planes. The allowable average shear stress of the pin is τ_a and the allowable bearing stress between pin and the central bar is σ_b. (a) Write the load limits P_shear and P_bearing in terms of d, t, τ_a, σ_b (use projected area d·t for bearing). (b) Find the ratio t/d at which both modes are critical simultaneously. (c) Explain physically why for t/d below this value bearing governs, and why average-stress values are only a design convention (what is the real distribution doing?).","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Double shear: two shear planes, each of area $\\pi d^2/4$, so $\\tau=\\dfrac{P/2}{\\pi d^2/4}=\\dfrac{2P}{\\pi d^2}$ and $P_{shear}=\\dfrac{\\pi d^2\\tau_a}{2}$.\n2. Bearing: contact pressure is non-uniform; use projected area $dt$: $\\sigma_b=P/(dt)$, so $P_{bearing}=d\\,t\\,\\sigma_b$.\n3. Equal limits: $\\dfrac{\\pi d^2\\tau_a}{2}=dt\\sigma_b\\Rightarrow \\dfrac{t}{d}=\\dfrac{\\pi\\tau_a}{2\\sigma_b}$.\n4. P_bearing is proportional to t, P_shear independent of t, so a thinner central bar (t/d smaller) has the lower bearing limit: bearing governs. Thicker bar: shear in the pin governs.\n5. Real distributions: shear stress in the pin is parabolic-ish and not uniform across d, contact pressure varies roughly cosine-like around the half-circumference, and bending of the pin also exists. Average values are calibrated against tests.\nProbe: single shear halves the shear area (P_shear = πd²τ_a/4). Which failure mode, tear-out/net-section tension of the plate, is not covered? (needs edge distance and net area.) Check dimensions: both P limits have units of force.","answers":["P_shear = πd²τ_a/2; P_bearing = d t σ_b","t/d = πτ_a/(2σ_b) for equal capacity","Thin central member: bearing governs; thick: pin shear governs"],"src":"Hibbeler §1.5 Average Shear Stress (pins in double shear, bearing)"},{"id":"1.3","title":"Stress on an inclined plane of an axial bar","q":"A prismatic bar of cross-sectional area A is loaded in axial tension P. Consider an imaginary plane cut whose normal makes an angle θ with the bar axis. (a) Derive the normal stress σ_θ and shear stress τ_θ on this plane in terms of σ = P/A and θ. (b) At what θ is the shear a maximum, and what are its value and the accompanying normal stress? (c) A glued scarf joint along this plane has allowable normal stress σ_g and allowable shear stress τ_g. Show how the allowable load P depends on θ and find the angle at which the two limits are equal. (d) Use τ_xy = τ_yx on an element to explain why the answer to (b) also appears on the orthogonal plane.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Plane area $A\u0027=A/\\cos\\theta$ (θ from axis to the plane normal). Force resolution: normal component $P\\cos\\theta$, tangential $P\\sin\\theta$.\n2. $\\sigma_\\theta=\\dfrac{P\\cos\\theta}{A/\\cos\\theta}=\\sigma\\cos^2\\theta$, $\\tau_\\theta=\\sigma\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta=\\tfrac12\\sigma\\sin2\\theta$.\n3. τ max at θ = 45°: $\\tau_{max}=\\sigma/2$, with σ_θ = σ/2 on that plane.\n4. Joint limits: $P\\le \\sigma_gA/\\cos^2\\theta$ and $P\\le \\tau_gA/(\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta)$. Equal when $\\tan\\theta=\\sigma_g/\\tau_g$. For smaller θ the normal-stress limit governs; for larger θ the shear limit.\n5. Moment equilibrium of an element $dx\\,dy$: $\\tau_{xy}\\,dy\\,dx=\\tau_{yx}\\,dx\\,dy$, so the shear on the perpendicular plane (θ+90°) has the same magnitude and the arrows meet or leave at a corner.\nProbe: why do mild-steel tensile specimens show slip lines at about 45°? (max shear plane). Check θ=0: σ_θ = σ, τ = 0 ✓.","answers":["σ_θ = σ cos²θ, τ_θ = σ sinθ cosθ = (σ/2) sin2θ","τ_max = σ/2 at θ = 45° (with σ_θ = σ/2)","Limits P ≤ σ_g A/cos²θ and P ≤ τ_g A/(sinθ cosθ); equal at tanθ = σ_g/τ_g"],"src":"Hibbeler §1.4 (stress on inclined sections of an axially loaded bar)"},{"id":"2.1","title":"Why a slack wire is nonlinear even when elastic","q":"A wire of unstretched length L, cross-sectional area A and modulus E is stretched taut between two rigid supports at the same level, with no initial tension. A transverse load P at midspan pulls the midpoint down by δ (δ ≪ L). (a) Find the normal strain in the wire exactly in terms of δ and L, then to leading order. (b) Using equilibrium of the midpoint, show that P is proportional to δ³ for small δ, and give the coefficient. (c) Explain why this is a geometric rather than a material nonlinearity, and why the small-angle theory of strain (strain ∝ displacement) cannot give this result.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Each half has length $L/2$ and drops δ: new length $\\sqrt{(L/2)^2+\\delta^2}$. Strain $\\varepsilon=\\dfrac{\\sqrt{(L/2)^2+\\delta^2}-L/2}{L/2}=\\sqrt{1+(2\\delta/L)^2}-1\\approx\\tfrac12(2\\delta/L)^2=2\\delta^2/L^2$.\n2. Tension $T=EA\\varepsilon$. Vertical equilibrium at the midpoint: $P=2T\\sin\\theta$ with $\\theta\\approx 2\\delta/L$. So $P\\approx 2EA\\cdot\\dfrac{2\\delta^2}{L^2}\\cdot\\dfrac{2\\delta}{L}=\\dfrac{8EA\\delta^3}{L^3}$.\n3. The material is linear (σ = Eε) but equilibrium must be written in the deformed geometry and ε is quadratic in δ. At δ = 0 the wire carries no load, so the transverse stiffness is zero initially and grows with δ² (stiffening, as for cables and membranes).\n4. Small-strain theory keeps first-order displacement gradients; here the first-order change of length is zero (displacement perpendicular to the wire), so the second-order term is the leading one.\nProbe: with initial tension T0 the result becomes P ≈ 4T0δ/L + 8EAδ³/L³. Check units: EA is a force.","answers":["ε = √(1+(2δ/L)²) − 1 ≈ 2δ²/L²","P ≈ 8EAδ³/L³ (P ∝ δ³)","Geometric nonlinearity: first-order elongation is zero for a transverse displacement, so strain is second order and equilibrium must use the deformed geometry"],"src":"Hibbeler §2.2 Strain (wire pulled at midspan)"},{"id":"2.2","title":"Simple shear: strain versus rotation","q":"A square element of side a deforms in simple shear: the bottom edge is fixed and every point at height y is displaced horizontally by u = γy, where γ ≪ 1 and the vertical displacement is zero. (a) Find the shear strain, and the normal strain of each diagonal of the square to first order. (b) Show that to first order the displacement can be split into a pure-shear deformation (zero rotation) plus a rigid rotation, and give the rotation angle. (c) Explain why a rigid-body rotation alone produces no strain, and what a strain gauge aligned with each diagonal would read.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. The right angle between the horizontal and vertical edges is reduced by $\\gamma_{xy}=\\partial u/\\partial y=\\gamma$ (small angle, $\\tan\\gamma\\approx\\gamma$). Horizontal edges keep their length; vertical edges tilt and lengthen only at second order: $\\varepsilon_x=\\varepsilon_y=0$.\n2. Diagonal through the origin, unit vector $(1,1)/\\sqrt2$: the far corner moves $(\\gamma a,0)$, elongation $=\\gamma a/\\sqrt2$, strain $=\\dfrac{\\gamma a/\\sqrt2}{\\sqrt2 a}=\\gamma/2$. Other diagonal (unit $(-1,1)/\\sqrt2$): strain $-\\gamma/2$. These match $\\varepsilon_{45}=\\tfrac12\\gamma_{xy}$ from strain transformation (Ch. 10): principal strains $\\pm\\gamma/2$ at 45°.\n3. Split: $(u,v)=(\\gamma y,0)=(\\tfrac\\gamma2 y,\\tfrac\\gamma2 x)+(\\tfrac\\gamma2 y,-\\tfrac\\gamma2 x)$. First term is pure shear (symmetric gradient, $\\gamma_{xy}=\\gamma$, no rotation). Second is a rigid rotation $\\omega=-\\gamma/2$ (clockwise; $u=-\\omega y,\\ v=\\omega x$).\n4. Rigid rotation gives $\\partial u/\\partial y+\\partial v/\\partial x=-\\omega+\\omega=0$ and $\\partial u/\\partial x=\\partial v/\\partial y=0$, so no strain: strain is the part of the displacement gradient that changes lengths and angles.\nProbe: exact diagonal length $\\sqrt{(a+\\gamma a)^2+a^2}$ shows the second-order correction. Gauges on the diagonals read +γ/2 and −γ/2.","answers":["γ_xy = γ; ε_x = ε_y = 0; diagonal strains +γ/2 and −γ/2","Displacement gradient = pure shear (γ_xy = γ) + rigid rotation of magnitude γ/2","Rigid rotation gives ∂u/∂y + ∂v/∂x = 0, so no strain"],"src":"Hibbeler §2.2 Strain; §10.1 Plane Strain"},{"id":"2.3","title":"Rigid bar on two wires: compatibility by similar triangles","q":"A rigid bar is hinged at A and held horizontal by two vertical wires of the same material and area. Wire 1 is attached at distance a from A and has length L1; wire 2 is attached at distance 2a from A and has length L2. A load is applied at distance 3a from A, rotating the bar through a small angle. (a) Find the ratio ε2/ε1 of the wire strains. (b) For what relation between L1 and L2 does wire 2 yield first? (c) If the wires are elastic-perfectly plastic with yield strain ε_Y, find the displacement of the load point when the first wire reaches yield.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Small rotation φ: wire displacements $\\delta_1=a\\varphi$, $\\delta_2=2a\\varphi$ (compatibility = similar triangles; the rigid bar stays straight).\n2. Strains $\\varepsilon_1=\\delta_1/L_1=a\\varphi/L_1$, $\\varepsilon_2=2a\\varphi/L_2$, so $\\dfrac{\\varepsilon_2}{\\varepsilon_1}=\\dfrac{2L_1}{L_2}$.\n3. Wire 2 yields first if $\\varepsilon_2\u003e\\varepsilon_1$, i.e. $L_2\u003c2L_1$. If $L_2\u003e2L_1$ wire 1 yields first. If $L_2=2L_1$ both together.\n4. At first yield: wire 2 first: $\\delta_2=\\varepsilon_YL_2$, $\\varphi=\\varepsilon_YL_2/(2a)$, load-point displacement $\\Delta=3a\\varphi=\\tfrac32\\varepsilon_YL_2$. Wire 1 first: $\\delta_1=\\varepsilon_YL_1$, $\\Delta=3\\varepsilon_YL_1$.\nProbe: why is small-angle geometry (δ ∝ distance from hinge) legitimate? Stresses (hence forces) in the wires are not equal; moment equilibrium would link them to the load. If E differed, the strain ratio is unchanged but the stress ratio changes.","answers":["ε2/ε1 = 2L1/L2","Wire 2 yields first when L2 \u003c 2L1 (wire 1 if L2 \u003e 2L1)","Load-point displacement at first yield: (3/2) ε_Y L2 if wire 2 first, 3 ε_Y L1 if wire 1 first"],"src":"Hibbeler §2.1-2.2 Deformation and strain (rigid bar supported by wires)"},{"id":"3.1","title":"Limits on Poisson\u0027s ratio","q":"An isotropic linear elastic bar of modulus E and Poisson\u0027s ratio ν is loaded in uniaxial tension σ. (a) Derive the volumetric strain (change in volume per unit volume) for small strains. (b) The same material is subjected to hydrostatic pressure p on all faces. Derive the bulk modulus K = p/(−e), where e is the volumetric strain, in terms of E and ν. (c) Using the relations G = E/[2(1+ν)] and K = E/[3(1−2ν)] and the requirement that G and K are positive (stable material), find the permissible range of ν. What does ν = 1/2 mean physically, and why is ν ≈ 0.5 for rubber and ν ≈ 0 for cork?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Uniaxial: $\\varepsilon_x=\\sigma/E$, $\\varepsilon_y=\\varepsilon_z=-\\nu\\sigma/E$. For small strains $e=\\varepsilon_x+\\varepsilon_y+\\varepsilon_z=\\dfrac{(1-2\\nu)\\sigma}{E}$ (from $(1+\\varepsilon_x)(1+\\varepsilon_y)(1+\\varepsilon_z)-1$, dropping products).\n2. Hydrostatic stress $\\sigma_x=\\sigma_y=\\sigma_z=-p$: by superposition each normal strain is $-\\dfrac{p}{E}(1-2\\nu)$, so $e=-\\dfrac{3(1-2\\nu)p}{E}$ and $K=\\dfrac{p}{-e}=\\dfrac{E}{3(1-2\\nu)}$.\n3. K \u003e 0 requires $\\nu\u003c1/2$ (with E \u003e 0). G \u003e 0 requires $\\nu\u003e-1$. So $-1\u003c\\nu\u003c\\tfrac12$ for isotropic stable materials; most real materials have $0\u003c\\nu\u003c\\tfrac12$.\n4. ν = 1/2: zero volume change (incompressible), K → ∞, G = E/3. Rubber has weak shear resistance but large bulk modulus, so ν ≈ 0.5. Cork\u0027s cells collapse axially without lateral bulging (ν ≈ 0), which is why it seals a bottle.\nProbe: what does ν \u003c 0 mean (auxetic foams swell laterally in tension)? Why does a plastic-flow material (volume constant) have ν = 1/2 in the plastic range?","answers":["e = (1−2ν)σ/E","K = E/[3(1−2ν)]","−1 \u003c ν \u003c 1/2 (usually 0 to 1/2); ν = 1/2 is incompressible, K → ∞"],"src":"Hibbeler §3.6 Poisson\u0027s Ratio; §10.6 Material-Property Relationships"},{"id":"3.2","title":"Why a reduced shank improves bolt energy absorption","q":"A bolt of total length L has a threaded portion of length L_t with root area A_t and an unthreaded shank of length L_s = L − L_t with area A_s \u003e A_t (E constant, linear elastic). Under axial load P: (a) derive the strain energy U in terms of P, E and the geometry. (b) Let the allowable stress σ_Y act in the weakest section. Find the maximum energy U₁ the original bolt can absorb elastically. (c) The shank is then turned down to the root area A_t. Find the new energy U₂ and the ratio U₂/U₁, and explain the conclusion in terms of modulus of resilience.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Series bars, same force: $U=\\sum\\dfrac{P^2L_i}{2A_iE}=\\dfrac{P^2}{2E}\\left(\\dfrac{L_s}{A_s}+\\dfrac{L_t}{A_t}\\right)$.\n2. The threaded root governs: $P_{max}=\\sigma_YA_t$. So $U_1=\\dfrac{\\sigma_Y^2A_t^2}{2E}\\left(\\dfrac{L_s}{A_s}+\\dfrac{L_t}{A_t}\\right)$.\n3. Shank at A_t: whole length stressed at σ_Y: $U_2=\\dfrac{\\sigma_Y^2A_t^2}{2E}\\dfrac{L}{A_t}=\\dfrac{\\sigma_Y^2}{2E}A_tL=u_rV$ with $u_r=\\sigma_Y^2/2E$ the modulus of resilience and V the volume.\n4. Ratio: $\\dfrac{U_2}{U_1}=\\dfrac{L}{L_t+L_sA_t/A_s}\u003e1$, tending to ∞ as the shank grows stiff relative to thread (A_s ≫ A_t) and L_s dominates.\nConclusion: energy capacity depends on how much of the material is stressed near the limit; an oversize shank is under-used. Reduced shank bolts are used in impact/vibration (see Ch. 14).\nProbe: the price: lower stiffness, a larger elongation at the same P; also the load capacity P_max is unchanged (σ_Y A_t).","answers":["U = (P²/2E)(L_s/A_s + L_t/A_t)","U₂ = σ_Y²A_tL/(2E) = u_r V; U₁ = (σ_Y²A_t²/2E)(L_s/A_s + L_t/A_t)","U₂/U₁ = L/(L_t + L_sA_t/A_s) \u003e 1: uniform stressing stores more energy for the same peak load"],"src":"Hibbeler §3.5 Strain Energy; §14.4 Impact Loading (bolt with a reduced shank)"},{"id":"3.3","title":"Necking from the true stress-strain curve","q":"A ductile bar is tested in tension. Engineering stress σ = P/A₀ and strain ε = ΔL/L₀ are tabulated, while true stress σ_t = P/A and true strain ε_t = ln(L/L₀) use instantaneous area and length. (a) Assuming constant volume during plastic flow, relate σ_t and ε_t to σ and ε. (b) Show that necking starts when dσ_t/dε_t = σ_t. (c) If the plastic curve is σ_t = Kε_t^n, find the true strain at the onset of necking and the corresponding engineering strain. (d) Why does the engineering curve fall after the ultimate load although the true stress keeps rising?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Constant volume: $AL=A_0L_0\\Rightarrow A=A_0/(1+\\varepsilon)$. So $\\sigma_t=\\dfrac P A=\\sigma(1+\\varepsilon)$ and $\\varepsilon_t=\\ln(1+\\varepsilon)$.\n2. $P=\\sigma_tA$. The ultimate load (start of necking) is where $dP=0$: $d\\sigma_t\\,A+\\sigma_t\\,dA=0$. Constant volume: $dA/A=-dL/L=-d\\varepsilon_t$. So $\\dfrac{d\\sigma_t}{d\\varepsilon_t}=\\sigma_t$ (Considère criterion).\n3. For $\\sigma_t=K\\varepsilon_t^n$: $nK\\varepsilon_t^{n-1}=K\\varepsilon_t^n\\Rightarrow\\varepsilon_t=n$. Then $\\varepsilon=e^n-1$.\n4. Engineering stress is load over original area. Beyond the peak, strain hardening ($d\\sigma_t$) can no longer compensate the area loss ($\\sigma_t\\,dA$): load drops, deformation localises in the neck, and the gauge-length averages hide that the neck section is still hardening. A true-stress curve needs the neck area, not the original.\nProbe: after necking, the stress state is triaxial in the neck (Bridgman correction). Why does perfect plasticity (n = 0) neck immediately upon yield?","answers":["σ_t = σ(1+ε), ε_t = ln(1+ε)","Necking when dσ_t/dε_t = σ_t (maximum load)","With σ_t = Kε_t^n: necking at ε_t = n (ε = eⁿ − 1)"],"src":"Hibbeler §3.2-3.3 Stress-strain diagram, ductile behaviour (true stress, necking)"},{"id":"4.1","title":"Heated core and sleeve between rigid plates","q":"A solid core (area A₁, modulus E₁, thermal expansion coefficient α₁) is surrounded by a coaxial sleeve (area A₂, E₂, α₂). Both have length L and are bonded to two rigid end plates, which are free to move (no external axial load). The assembly is heated uniformly by ΔT. (a) Derive the axial force P₁ in the core (tension positive) and the common elongation δ. (b) Check the limits α₁ = α₂ and A₂E₂ → ∞. (c) Now suppose instead that the end plates are held a fixed distance L apart. Find the reaction force on the plates and the force in each member.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Equilibrium (free plates, no external load): $P_1+P_2=0$.\n2. Compatibility: same elongation δ: $\\delta=\\dfrac{P_1L}{A_1E_1}+\\alpha_1\\Delta TL=-\\dfrac{P_1L}{A_2E_2}+\\alpha_2\\Delta TL$.\n3. Solve: $P_1=\\dfrac{(\\alpha_2-\\alpha_1)\\Delta T\\,A_1E_1A_2E_2}{A_1E_1+A_2E_2}$, $P_2=-P_1$, and $\\delta=\\dfrac{\\alpha_1A_1E_1+\\alpha_2A_2E_2}{A_1E_1+A_2E_2}\\Delta TL$ (a stiffness-weighted average of the free expansions).\n4. Limits: α₁ = α₂ gives P₁ = 0 (free expansion, no stress). $A_2E_2\\to\\infty$ gives $P_1\\to(\\alpha_2-\\alpha_1)\\Delta TA_1E_1$ and δ → α₂ΔTL: the stiff sleeve dictates the expansion and the core is forced to follow it.\n5. Fixed plates (δ = 0): each member is fully restrained, $P_i=-\\alpha_i\\Delta TA_iE_i$ (compressive), and the plates exert total reaction $F=\\Delta T(\\alpha_1A_1E_1+\\alpha_2A_2E_2)$; now $P_1+P_2\\ne0$ because the supports carry load.\nProbe: if α₂ \u003e α₁ the core is in tension and the sleeve in compression (the core is dragged along).","answers":["P₁ = (α₂−α₁)ΔT A₁E₁A₂E₂/(A₁E₁+A₂E₂), P₂ = −P₁","δ = (α₁A₁E₁+α₂A₂E₂)ΔT L/(A₁E₁+A₂E₂)","Fixed plates: P_i = −α_iΔT A_iE_i and reaction F = ΔT(α₁A₁E₁+α₂A₂E₂)"],"src":"Hibbeler §4.4-4.6 Statically indeterminate axial members; Thermal Stress"},{"id":"4.2","title":"Residual stress at a hole after plastic overload","q":"A thin plate with a hole is loaded in tension. K is the stress concentration factor defined on the net section: σ_max = K σ_avg with σ_avg = P/A_net. The material is elastic-perfectly plastic with yield stress σ_Y. (a) Find the load P_Y at which the hole edge first yields, and the load P_p at which the whole net section is yielded. What is P_p/P_Y? (b) After loading to P_p the plate is unloaded elastically (assume the unloading stress distribution follows the elastic K pattern). Find the residual stress at the hole edge and the condition for no reverse yielding. (c) Explain why the average residual stress on the net section is zero and what this means for subsequent cyclic loading.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. First yield at the edge: $K\\dfrac{P_Y}{A_{net}}=\\sigma_Y\\Rightarrow P_Y=\\dfrac{\\sigma_YA_{net}}{K}$.\n2. Full plastic: the whole net section at σ_Y: $P_p=\\sigma_YA_{net}$ (limit load independent of K). $P_p/P_Y=K$.\n3. Unloading from P_p is elastic with the K-pattern: edge stress change $-K\\dfrac{P_p}{A_{net}}=-K\\sigma_Y$. Residual edge stress $\\sigma_r=\\sigma_Y-K\\sigma_Y=-(K-1)\\sigma_Y$ (compressive).\n4. Reverse yielding is avoided if $(K-1)\\sigma_Y\\le\\sigma_Y$, i.e. $K\\le2$ (compressive yield −σ_Y); if K \u003e 2 the edge yields again on unloading.\n5. Residual stress is self-equilibrated: unloading removes net force $P_p$ and the elastic change integrates to exactly $-P_p$, so $\\int\\sigma_rdA=0$; the compressive edge is balanced by tension elsewhere on the net section. The compressive residual stress at the hole edge is beneficial for fatigue (as in cold-working holes).\nProbe: why is the limit load for a ductile material insensitive to the notch, while a brittle material fails near P_Y?","answers":["P_Y = σ_Y A_net/K; P_p = σ_Y A_net; ratio K","Residual edge stress = −(K−1)σ_Y; no reverse yield for K ≤ 2","Residual stress is self-equilibrated (zero net force); compressive at hole edge helps fatigue"],"src":"Hibbeler §4.7 Stress Concentrations; §4.8-4.9 Inelastic Axial Deformation, Residual Stress"},{"id":"4.3","title":"Parallel members of different yield strain","q":"Two prismatic bars of length L are attached to a rigid plate and share an axial load P. Bar 1 has area A₁, modulus E₁, yield stress σ_Y1; bar 2 has A₂, E₂, σ_Y2. Both are elastic-perfectly plastic. Assume yield strains are ordered ε_Y1 = σ_Y1/E₁ \u003c ε_Y2 = σ_Y2/E₂. (a) Find the load P_Y at which the first bar yields and the limit load P_p. (b) After loading to P_p the load is removed. Derive the residual stress in each bar and verify that the residual forces sum to zero. (c) Explain why the unloading is elastic with stiffness (A₁E₁ + A₂E₂)/L regardless of the yielded state.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Same displacement implies the same strain ε in both bars (compatibility). Bar 1 yields first at $\\varepsilon=\\varepsilon_{Y1}$: $P_Y=A_1\\sigma_{Y1}+A_2E_2\\varepsilon_{Y1}$.\n2. Beyond ε_Y2 both are yielded: $P_p=A_1\\sigma_{Y1}+A_2\\sigma_{Y2}$ (strain at P_p equals ε_Y2).\n3. Unloading: both bars follow their elastic lines (slopes E_i) with the same strain change Δε. Force change $-P_p=(A_1E_1+A_2E_2)\\Delta\\varepsilon$ so $\\Delta\\varepsilon=-\\dfrac{P_p}{A_1E_1+A_2E_2}$.\n4. Residual: $\\sigma_{r1}=\\sigma_{Y1}+E_1\\Delta\\varepsilon=\\sigma_{Y1}-\\dfrac{E_1P_p}{A_1E_1+A_2E_2}$, $\\sigma_{r2}=\\sigma_{Y2}-\\dfrac{E_2P_p}{A_1E_1+A_2E_2}$.\n5. Check: $A_1\\sigma_{r1}+A_2\\sigma_{r2}=P_p-P_p=0$ ✓. Plastic strain is permanent, so unloading is elastic with the elastic stiffness of the parallel pair.\nProbe: at least one bar is left in compression and the other in tension; if the yield strains are equal the residual stresses vanish.","answers":["P_Y = A₁σ_Y1 + A₂E₂ε_Y1; P_p = A₁σ_Y1 + A₂σ_Y2","σ_r1 = σ_Y1 − E₁P_p/(A₁E₁+A₂E₂); σ_r2 = σ_Y2 − E₂P_p/(A₁E₁+A₂E₂)","Residual forces sum to zero; unloading uses stiffness (A₁E₁+A₂E₂)/L"],"src":"Hibbeler §4.8 Inelastic Axial Deformation; §4.9 Residual Stress"},{"id":"5.1","title":"Fully plastic shaft and its residual stress","q":"A solid circular shaft of radius c is made of an elastic-perfectly plastic material with shear yield stress τ_Y and shear modulus G. (a) Derive the torque T_Y at first yield and the torque T when the elastic core has radius ρ_Y. (b) Derive the fully plastic torque T_p and the ratio T_p/T_Y. (c) The shaft is twisted to T_p and then unloaded. Derive the residual shear stress at the surface and show the residual stress distribution carries zero net torque. (d) Explain why the shaft retains a permanent twist after unloading.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Elastic: τ = Tρ/J, J = πc⁴/2. First yield at the surface: $T_Y=\\dfrac{\\tau_YJ}{c}=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\tau_Yc^3$.\n2. Partial yield: τ = τ_Yρ/ρ_Y for ρ ≤ ρ_Y, τ = τ_Y outside. $T=\\int\\tau\\rho\\,dA=\\int_0^{\\rho_Y}\\dfrac{\\tau_Y}{\\rho_Y}\\rho^2\\,2\\pi\\rho\\,d\\rho+\\int_{\\rho_Y}^{c}\\tau_Y\\rho\\,2\\pi\\rho\\,d\\rho=\\dfrac{\\pi\\tau_Y}{6}(4c^3-\\rho_Y^3)$.\n3. ρ_Y → 0: $T_p=\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\\tau_Yc^3=\\dfrac43T_Y$.\n4. Unloading is elastic: removing T_p gives a linear stress with surface value $\\dfrac{T_pc}{J}=\\dfrac43\\tau_Y$. Residual: $\\tau_r(\\rho)=\\tau_Y-\\dfrac43\\tau_Y\\dfrac\\rho c$, so at the surface $-\\tau_Y/3$, zero at ρ = 3c/4, +τ_Y at the centre. Net torque: $\\int\\tau_r\\rho\\,dA=T_p-T_p=0$ ✓.\n5. Plastic shear strains are not recovered; the fibres cannot all return to zero strain without conflicting with residual stress, so a permanent twist remains.\nProbe: check ρ_Y = c gives T_Y. What is the permanent twist? (φ_p − T_pL/GJ.) Why is surface residual stress compressive? (Surface strains were highest, so unload from greatest strain.)","answers":["T_Y = πτ_Yc³/2; T = (π/6)τ_Y(4c³ − ρ_Y³)","T_p = (2π/3)τ_Yc³ = (4/3)T_Y","Residual surface stress = −τ_Y/3; τ_r = τ_Y(1 − 4ρ/(3c)); zero net torque"],"src":"Hibbeler §5.9 Inelastic Torsion; §5.10 Residual Stress"},{"id":"5.2","title":"Closed versus open thin-walled tube in torsion","q":"A thin circular tube of mean radius r and wall thickness t (t ≪ r) carries torque T. (a) For the closed tube derive the average shear stress in terms of T, r, t using the shear-flow concept and the torsional constant J_c. (b) Now the tube is slit along its length (open section). Treat the open section as a thin rectangle of length 2πr and thickness t and give τ_max and J_o. (c) Find the ratio T_closed/T_open for the same maximum shear stress and the ratio of torsional stiffnesses J_c/J_o. (d) Explain physically why the slit tube is so much weaker, using the direction of the shear flow.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Closed tube: constant shear flow $q=\\tau t$, torque $T=2A_mq$ with $A_m=\\pi r^2$: $\\tau=\\dfrac{T}{2\\pi r^2t}$. Twist rate gives $J_c=\\dfrac{4A_m^2t}{\\oint ds}=\\dfrac{4\\pi^2r^4t}{2\\pi r}=2\\pi r^3t$ (agrees with the thin-wall limit of π(c_o⁴ − c_i⁴)/2 ≈ 2πr³t).\n2. Open (slit) section: thin rectangle, $J_o=\\dfrac13bt^3=\\dfrac{2\\pi rt^3}{3}$, $\\tau_{max}=\\dfrac{Tt}{J_o}=\\dfrac{3T}{2\\pi rt^2}$.\n3. Same τ: $T_{closed}=2\\pi r^2t\\tau$, $T_{open}=\\dfrac{2\\pi rt^2}{3}\\tau$, so $\\dfrac{T_c}{T_o}=\\dfrac{3r}{t}$. Stiffness ratio $\\dfrac{J_c}{J_o}=\\dfrac{3r^2}{t^2}$ (a tube with r/t = 20 is 1200 times stiffer).\n4. Physical: in the closed tube the shear flow circulates around the wall at lever arm ≈ r, so each element of material works at large moment arm. In the slit tube the flow must go along one side and return on the other within the thickness t, so the lever arm is of order t.\nProbe: in the slit tube the shear stress distribution across t is linear (zero at mid thickness). Why does a slit also destroy warping restraint? Limiting case t → r: ratios tend to 3.","answers":["Closed: τ = T/(2πr²t), J_c = 2πr³t","Open: J_o = 2πr t³/3, τ_max = 3T/(2πr t²)","T_c/T_o = 3r/t (same τ); J_c/J_o = 3r²/t²"],"src":"Hibbeler §5.7 Thin-Walled Tubes Having Closed Cross Sections; §5.6 Solid Noncircular Shafts"},{"id":"5.3","title":"Two-segment shaft: reactions and collapse torque","q":"A solid circular shaft is fixed at both ends A and B. Segment AC (length L₁, radius c₁) and segment CB (length L₂, radius c₂) have the same shear modulus G. A torque T₀ is applied at C. (a) Using compatibility of twist at C, derive the reactions T_A and T_B in terms of L₁, L₂, c₁, c₂. (b) Check the case c₁ = c₂ with L₁ = L₂. (c) The material is elastic-perfectly plastic with shear yield τ_Y. Find the torque T₀ at which both segments become fully plastic (collapse), and comment on its dependence on L₁ and L₂.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Equilibrium: $T_A+T_B=T_0$. Compatibility: twist of C from A equals twist from B (both ends fixed): $\\dfrac{T_AL_1}{GJ_1}=\\dfrac{T_BL_2}{GJ_2}$ with $J_i=\\pi c_i^4/2$.\n2. Solve: $T_A=T_0\\dfrac{L_2J_1}{L_2J_1+L_1J_2}=T_0\\dfrac{L_2c_1^4}{L_2c_1^4+L_1c_2^4}$, $T_B=T_0\\dfrac{L_1c_2^4}{L_2c_1^4+L_1c_2^4}$. The stiffer/shorter segment takes more torque (torsional stiffness GJ/L acts like parallel springs).\n3. Check: equal shafts and L₁ = L₂ gives T_A = T_B = T₀/2. L₁ → 0 gives T_A → T₀ (C is welded to A).\n4. Collapse: each segment fully plastic with $T_{p,i}=\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\\tau_Yc_i^3$. At collapse $T_A=T_{p,1}$, $T_B=T_{p,2}$: $T_{0,p}=\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\\tau_Y(c_1^3+c_2^3)$, independent of L₁ and L₂ (plastic limit analysis needs equilibrium only; lengths only decide the elastic sharing and when yield starts).\nProbe: which segment yields first in the elastic range? (compare $T_ic_i/J_i$, i.e. $T_i/c_i^3$.) Ratio of collapse torque to first-yield torque is \u003e1.","answers":["T_A = T₀ L₂c₁⁴/(L₂c₁⁴ + L₁c₂⁴); T_B = T₀ L₁c₂⁴/(L₂c₁⁴ + L₁c₂⁴)","Equal shafts, L₁ = L₂: T_A = T_B = T₀/2","Collapse torque T₀,p = (2π/3)τ_Y(c₁³ + c₂³), independent of lengths"],"src":"Hibbeler §5.5 Statically Indeterminate Torque-Loaded Members; §5.9 Inelastic Torsion"},{"id":"6.1","title":"Rectangular beam taken past yield","q":"A beam of rectangular cross-section (width b, depth h) is made of an elastic-perfectly plastic material with yield stress σ_Y (same in tension and compression) and bends about its horizontal centroidal axis. (a) Derive the yield moment M_Y and the fully plastic moment M_p, and the shape factor M_p/M_Y. (b) The moment M_p is applied and then removed. Derive the residual stress distribution and the residual stress at the top and bottom surfaces. (c) Explain why the plastic neutral axis for an unsymmetric section is the equal-area axis, not the centroidal axis, and say what this means for a T-section.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Elastic: $\\sigma=My/I$, $I=bh^3/12$. First yield at $y=h/2$: $M_Y=\\dfrac{\\sigma_YI}{h/2}=\\dfrac{bh^2}{6}\\sigma_Y$.\n2. Fully plastic: stress ±σ_Y over each half. Tension and compression resultants each $\\sigma_Y\\,bh/2$ at lever arm $h/2$: $M_p=\\dfrac{bh^2}{4}\\sigma_Y$. Shape factor $f=\\dfrac{M_p}{M_Y}=\\dfrac32$.\n3. Unloading: elastic, stress change $-M_py/I$; at the surface $-\\dfrac{bh^2\\sigma_Y/4}{bh^3/12}\\cdot\\dfrac h2=-\\dfrac32\\sigma_Y$. The elastic change at height y is $-3\\sigma_Yy/h$, so for y \u003e 0: $\\sigma_r=\\sigma_Y(1-3y/h)$. At the top surface $y=h/2$ this is $-\\sigma_Y/2$ (compression), zero at $y=h/3$, and +σ_Y as $y\\to0^+$ (antisymmetric below the axis). Net force and moment are zero.\n4. For the fully plastic section $\\sum F=0$: $\\sigma_YA_t=\\sigma_YA_c$, so $A_t=A_c$: the plastic neutral axis divides the area in two equal halves. For a T-section this lies in the web or flange away from the elastic centroid, so the section moves its neutral axis as yielding progresses.\nCheck: elastic-core result $M=\\tfrac32M_Y\\left[1-\\tfrac13(y_Y/c)^2\\right]$, c=h/2, goes to M_Y at y_Y = c and 1.5M_Y at 0.\nProbe: shape factors: circle 16/3π ≈ 1.70, thin I ≈ 1.","answers":["M_Y = bh²σ_Y/6; M_p = bh²σ_Y/4; shape factor 3/2","Residual σ_r = σ_Y(1 − 3y/h) for 0 \u003c y ≤ h/2; surface value −σ_Y/2; zero at y = h/3","Plastic neutral axis divides the area into equal halves (A_t = A_c)"],"src":"Hibbeler §6.10 Inelastic Bending (plastic moment, residual stress)"},{"id":"6.2","title":"Neutral axis orientation in unsymmetric bending","q":"A beam with a cross-section whose principal centroidal axes are y and z (principal moments I_y and I_z) carries a bending moment of magnitude M whose vector makes an angle θ with the z axis (so the moment components are M_z = M cosθ and M_y = M sinθ). (a) Derive the equation of the neutral axis and its angle α with the z axis, in terms of I_y, I_z, θ. (b) For which cases does the neutral axis coincide with the load direction? (c) A deep narrow rectangular section has I_z ≫ I_y. Describe where the neutral axis lies for a small θ and what it means for the deflection direction and the point of maximum stress.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Superposition about principal axes: $\\sigma=-\\dfrac{M_zy}{I_z}+\\dfrac{M_yz}{I_y}$ (signs chosen so that M_z causes compression at y \u003e 0).\n2. Neutral axis σ = 0: $\\dfrac{y}{z}=\\dfrac{M_yI_z}{M_zI_y}=\\dfrac{I_z}{I_y}\\tan\\theta$, a straight line through the centroid at angle α with $\\tan\\alpha=\\dfrac{I_z}{I_y}\\tan\\theta$.\n3. α = θ if $I_y=I_z$ (circle, square, any section with equal principal moments, so the NA is perpendicular to the moment plane and the beam bends in the plane of load) or if θ = 0, 90° (moment about a principal axis). Otherwise the NA does not coincide with the load direction, and deflection is not in the plane of the load: it is perpendicular to the neutral axis (curvature vector in the direction conjugate to NA).\n4. I_z ≫ I_y: $\\tan\\alpha\\gg\\tan\\theta$; even a tiny θ makes α large, so the NA swings toward the y axis (vertical), most of the section is stressed by the weak-axis bending and the deflection is mainly sideways. Maximum stress occurs at the corner farthest from the NA. This explains why deep narrow joists must be braced against small load eccentricity.\nProbe: check θ small, I_z/I_y = 25: α = 25θ for small angles, until tanα saturates; for θ = 2°, α ≈ 41°.","answers":["Neutral axis: y/z = (I_z/I_y) tanθ, passing through the centroid","tanα = (I_z/I_y) tanθ; α = θ only if I_y = I_z or θ = 0°, 90°","Deep narrow section: NA is far from the load plane for tiny θ; strong lateral deflection and high corner stress"],"src":"Hibbeler §6.5 Unsymmetric Bending"},{"id":"6.3","title":"Neutral axis of a curved rectangular beam","q":"A beam of rectangular cross-section (depth h, width b) is curved in its plane with inner radius r_i and outer radius r_o = r_i + h; r̄ = (r_i + r_o)/2 is the radius of the centroid. For a bending moment M the Winkler (curved beam) stress is σ = M(R − r)/(A e r) with r the radius of the fibre, A the area, R the radius of the neutral axis and e = r̄ − R. (a) Derive R for the rectangle: R = h/ln(r_o/r_i). (b) Show that as h/r̄ → 0 the offset e → h²/(12 r̄) and the stress tends to My/I. (c) Explain qualitatively why the neutral axis lies between the centroid and the centre of curvature and why the maximum stress occurs on the inner fibre.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Plane sections remain plane. Fibre length at radius r is proportional to r, so the strain is $\\varepsilon=k\\,(r-R)/r$. Zero net force: $\\int\\sigma\\,dA=0\\Rightarrow\\int\\dfrac{r-R}{r}dA=0\\Rightarrow R=\\dfrac{A}{\\int dA/r}$.\n2. Rectangle: $A=bh$, $\\int dA/r=b\\ln(r_o/r_i)$, so $R=\\dfrac{h}{\\ln(r_o/r_i)}$.\n3. Limit: $x=h/(2\\bar r)$, $\\ln\\dfrac{1+x}{1-x}=2x+\\tfrac23x^3+\\dots$, so $R=\\dfrac{h}{2x(1+x^2/3)}\\approx\\bar r(1-x^2/3)$ and $e=\\bar r-R\\approx\\dfrac{\\bar rx^2}{3}=\\dfrac{h^2}{12\\bar r}=\\dfrac{I}{A\\bar r}$. Then $\\sigma\\approx\\dfrac{M(R-r)}{A\\,(I/A\\bar r)\\,\\bar r}=\\dfrac{M(R-r)}{I}$, i.e. My/I with y = R − r measured from the neutral axis (centroid in the limit).\n4. Inner fibres have a shorter length but the same angular rotation of sections, so strain is larger there (strain ∝ (r−R)/r, hyperbolic): the stress is higher at the inner side, and the neutral axis shifts toward the centre of curvature to maintain zero net force (more area outside).\nProbe: for r̄/h = 1, is the straight-beam formula within 10%? Why does a hook fail on the inside?","answers":["R = A/∫(dA/r) = h/ln(r_o/r_i)","e = r̄ − R → h²/(12 r̄) = I/(A r̄) as h/r̄ → 0; σ → My/I","Neutral axis shifts toward the centre of curvature; hyperbolic stress, largest at the inner fibre"],"src":"Hibbeler §6.8 Curved Beams"},{"id":"7.1","title":"Where the shear formula comes from, and its limits","q":"A straight prismatic beam of rectangular cross-section (width b, depth h, constant) carries an internal shear V (dV/dx may be nonzero). (a) By equilibrium of a short segment of length dx cut horizontally at height y above the neutral axis, derive the shear flow q = VQ/I and the shear stress τ = VQ/(It). (b) Evaluate τ(y) for the rectangle, give τ_max in terms of V and A = bh, and verify that ∫τ dA = V. (c) Why is τ zero at the top and bottom surfaces, and why is the formula unreliable for a very wide, shallow section (b \u003e h) and near a flange-web junction?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Take the portion above y. Moment M and M + dM differ across dx, so the normal forces on the two ends differ by $dF=\\dfrac{dM}{I}\\int_{A\u0027}y\\,dA=\\dfrac{dM}{I}Q$ with $Q=\\int_{A\u0027}y\\,dA$. Horizontal equilibrium needs a shear force $dF=q\\,dx$ on the cut: $q=\\dfrac{dM}{dx}\\dfrac QI=\\dfrac{VQ}{I}$. Dividing by the cut width t (assuming τ uniform across t): $\\tau=\\dfrac{VQ}{It}$.\n2. Rectangle: $Q=\\dfrac b2(c^2-y^2)$, c = h/2, I = bh³/12: $\\tau=\\dfrac{V}{2I}(c^2-y^2)=\\dfrac{3V}{2A}\\left(1-\\dfrac{y^2}{c^2}\\right)$. $\\tau_{max}=\\dfrac{3V}{2A}$ (1.5 × average). Check: $\\int_{-c}^{c}\\tau\\,b\\,dy=\\dfrac{3V}{4c}\\left(2c-\\dfrac{2c}{3}\\right)=V$ ✓.\n3. Complementary shear: $\\tau_{xy}=\\tau_{yx}$; the free top and bottom surfaces have no shear traction, so τ = 0 there (Q = 0).\n4. Assumption of uniform τ across width and of plane sections (no warping) is poor when b ≳ h (stress rises at the edges, which is why 3V/2A is a poor estimate for a plate), and at a junction where the width t changes abruptly (stress concentration, flow turning).\nProbe: circular section gives 4V/3A. For a thin I-beam web carries about 90 per cent of V.","answers":["q = VQ/I, τ = VQ/(It) from dF = (dM/I)Q = q dx","Rectangle: τ = (3V/2A)(1 − y²/c²), τ_max = 3V/(2A), ∫τ dA = V","Fails when width is comparable to depth (non-uniform τ across width) and at abrupt width changes"],"src":"Hibbeler §7.1-7.2 Shear in Straight Members; The Shear Formula"},{"id":"7.2","title":"Shear centre of a semicircular open section","q":"A thin-walled open section has the shape of a semicircular arc of mean radius r and constant thickness t (t ≪ r). The arc is the half of a circle on one side of a diameter; its centre of curvature is O (the midpoint of the open side) and the two ends of the arc lie on the diameter through O. A shear force V acts in the plane of the section, parallel to the diameter joining the arc ends (perpendicular to the arc\u0027s symmetry axis). (a) Derive the shear flow q(φ) around the arc using q = VQ/I, with φ the angle at O measured from the symmetry axis (−π/2 ≤ φ ≤ π/2); the neutral axis is the symmetry axis. (b) Take moments about O to locate the shear centre and give its distance e from O and on which side it lies. (c) Compare with the shear centre of a thin channel (distance e = 3b²/(h+6b) from the web) and say why both lie outside the section.","figs":[],"figDesc":"Thin semicircular arc of mean radius r and thickness t; centre O on the open side midpoint; shear force V parallel to the line through the arc ends. No dimensions beyond r, t.","sol":"1. V is perpendicular to the symmetry axis, so the neutral axis is the symmetry axis (it passes through the centroid and through O). Measure φ from it, −π/2 ≤ φ ≤ π/2, so a point on the arc is at height $y=r\\sin\\varphi$ from the neutral axis and $ds=r\\,d\\varphi$. $I=\\int y^2t\\,ds=tr^3\\int_{-\\pi/2}^{\\pi/2}\\sin^2\\varphi\\,d\\varphi=\\dfrac{\\pi r^3t}{2}$.\n2. Q for the part of the arc between the free end (φ = −π/2) and the section at φ: $Q=\\int y\\,t\\,ds=tr^2\\int_{-\\pi/2}^{\\varphi}\\sin\\varphi\u0027\\,d\\varphi\u0027=-tr^2\\cos\\varphi$, magnitude $tr^2\\cos\\varphi$ (zero at the free ends, as required). So $q=\\dfrac{VQ}{I}=\\dfrac{2V}{\\pi r}\\cos\\varphi$.\n3. Check: vertical resultant $\\int q\\cos\\varphi\\,r\\,d\\varphi=\\dfrac{2V}{\\pi}\\int\\cos^2\\varphi\\,d\\varphi=V$ ✓.\n4. Moments about O: each element\u0027s force $q\\,r\\,d\\varphi$ is tangential, lever arm r: $M_O=\\int qr^2d\\varphi=\\dfrac{2Vr}{\\pi}\\int_{-\\pi/2}^{\\pi/2}\\cos\\varphi\\,d\\varphi=\\dfrac{4Vr}{\\pi}$. Set Ve = 4Vr/π: $e=\\dfrac{4r}{\\pi}\\approx1.27r$ from O, on the side of the arc (beyond the arc, 0.27 r outside it).\n5. As with the channel, the shear flow along the \u0027web\u0027 (here the arc crown) acts mostly in direction V while the flange-like ends carry opposite-direction horizontal flows; the resultant of V must pass outside the section so that the flow pair twist cancels.\nProbe: thin ring slit: e = 2r. Check units: e is a length.","answers":["q = (2V/πr) cos φ (φ from the symmetry axis, |φ| ≤ π/2); I = πr³t/2","Shear centre at e = 4r/π from O, on the arc side (beyond the arc)","Like the channel, it lies outside the section on the side of the web"],"src":"Hibbeler §7.5 Shear Center for Open Thin-Walled Members"},{"id":"7.3","title":"Fastener count in a built-up beam","q":"A simply supported built-up beam of span L carries a uniform load w. Two members are connected along a horizontal interface by identical fasteners, each able to transmit a shear force F. The static moment of the connected part about the neutral axis is Q and the moment of inertia of the whole section is I (constant). (a) Find the required fastener spacing s(x) at a distance x from the left support. (b) Find the total number N of fasteners required over the whole span, assuming spacing can vary continuously. (c) Show that N depends only on the maximum bending moment and give a physical interpretation. (d) Why can the spacing not actually be made infinite at midspan?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Shear flow at the interface: $q=\\dfrac{VQ}{I}$ (force per unit length). A fastener at spacing s carries $qs\\le F$, so $s(x)=\\dfrac{FI}{V(x)Q}$ with $V(x)=w\\left(\\dfrac L2-x\\right)$ for 0 ≤ x ≤ L/2. s → ∞ at midspan where V = 0.\n2. Number per unit length is $1/s=\\dfrac{QV}{FI}$. For one half span: $N_{1/2}=\\int_0^{L/2}\\dfrac{QV}{FI}dx=\\dfrac{Q}{FI}\\cdot\\dfrac{wL^2}{8}$. Both halves (V is symmetric in magnitude): $N=\\dfrac{QwL^2}{4FI}$.\n3. Since $\\int_0^{L/2}V\\,dx=M_{max}=\\dfrac{wL^2}{8}$: $N=\\dfrac{2QM_{max}}{FI}$. Physically $QM/I$ is the total longitudinal force $\\int\\sigma\\,dA\u0027$ the connected part must transfer to reach the peak moment, so N × F equals twice the end force at midspan (once per half).\n4. In practice the minimum spacing is set by the code (spacing limits, minimum fasteners, shear from concentrated loads, uplift etc.), not zero; the formula gives the minimum strength-based number (rounded up). Also, V changes sign only at midspan, where the formula\u0027s assumption of slowly varying spacing breaks down.\nProbe: dimensions: [Q] = L³, [I]= L⁴, wL²/F dimensionless → N dimensionless ✓. Doubling span quadruples N.","answers":["s(x) = FI/(V(x)Q) with V = w(L/2 − x) on the left half; infinite at midspan","N = wQL²/(4FI) = 2Q M_max/(FI)","N is proportional to the area under the shear diagram (= M_max per half) because Q/I times that moment is the total force to be transferred"],"src":"Hibbeler §7.3 Shear Flow in Built-Up Members"},{"id":"8.1","title":"Thin vessel: wall stresses and the head-shell mismatch","q":"A closed thin-walled cylindrical vessel has mean radius r, wall thickness t (t ≪ r) and internal gauge pressure p. (a) By free-body diagrams derive the hoop stress σ_h and the axial stress σ_a in the cylinder wall, and the stress σ_s in a thin spherical shell of the same r, t, p. (b) Explain why a cylindrical boiler splits along a longitudinal seam first, and why the thin-wall formulas ignore the radial stress. (c) The cylinder is closed by hemispherical heads of the same r and t. Compare the radial growth of the cylinder and of the hemisphere (use Hooke\u0027s law, Poisson\u0027s ratio ν) and say what this implies at the junction.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Cut the cylinder along a diametral plane (length L): $2\\sigma_htL=p(2rL)\\Rightarrow\\sigma_h=\\dfrac{pr}{t}$. Cut transversely: $\\sigma_a(2\\pi rt)=p\\pi r^2\\Rightarrow\\sigma_a=\\dfrac{pr}{2t}$. Sphere (cut through the centre): $\\sigma_s(2\\pi rt)=p\\pi r^2\\Rightarrow\\sigma_s=\\dfrac{pr}{2t}$.\n2. Hoop stress is twice the axial stress, so a longitudinal seam (carrying hoop tension) fails before a circumferential one. The radial stress varies from −p (inner) to 0 (outer): its value is of order p, small compared with pr/t when r/t ≫ 1 (r/t ≥ 10 is the usual criterion).\n3. Radial growth Δr = rε_h. Cylinder: $\\varepsilon_h=\\dfrac{1}{E}(\\sigma_h-\\nu\\sigma_a)=\\dfrac{pr}{2tE}(2-\\nu)$. Sphere: $\\varepsilon=\\dfrac1E(\\sigma_s-\\nu\\sigma_s)=\\dfrac{pr}{2tE}(1-\\nu)$. The cylinder grows more (by a factor (2−ν)/(1−ν) ≈ 1.2 for ν = 0.3).\n4. Since the shells are joined, compatibility forces local radial shear and bending at the junction (discontinuity stresses), which are not given by membrane theory and are the reason heads are made thicker or have a knuckle/torispherical shape. The effect decays over a length of order √(rt).\nProbe: why is the spherical shape the most efficient (half the stress)? Check: with open ends, σ_a = 0.","answers":["σ_h = pr/t, σ_a = pr/(2t) (cylinder); σ_s = pr/(2t) (sphere)","Hoop stress is twice axial, so longitudinal seam fails first; radial stress is ≤ p, negligible for r/t ≥ 10","Cylinder grows more than a same-thickness hemisphere by (2−ν)/(1−ν), causing discontinuity bending stresses at the junction"],"src":"Hibbeler §8.1 Thin-Walled Pressure Vessels"},{"id":"8.2","title":"Core (kern) of a column section","q":"A short column of cross-sectional area A and centroidal radius of gyration r_g (I = A r_g² about the bending axis) carries a compressive load P applied with eccentricity e from the centroid along a principal axis; c is the distance from the centroid to the extreme fibre on the side of the load. (a) Write the maximum and minimum normal stresses and find the largest e (the kern limit) for which no tension occurs. (b) Evaluate it for a rectangle of depth h (bending in the depth direction) and a solid circle of radius R. (c) For a rectangle b × h with load eccentric in both directions (e_y, e_z) find the shape of the kern region, and explain why it matters for masonry/concrete and for soil under footings.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Superposition: $\\sigma=-\\dfrac PA\\mp\\dfrac{Pec}{I}=-\\dfrac PA\\left(1\\pm\\dfrac{ec}{r_g^2}\\right)$ (compression at the loaded side).\n2. No tension at the far fibre: $1-\\dfrac{ec}{r_g^2}\\ge0\\Rightarrow e\\le\\dfrac{r_g^2}{c}=\\dfrac{I}{Ac}$.\n3. Rectangle, $r_g^2=h^2/12$, c = h/2: $e\\le h/6$ (middle third). Circle, $r_g^2=R^2/4$, c = R: $e\\le R/4$ (middle quarter of the diameter, i.e. a circle of radius R/4).\n4. Biaxial: $\\sigma=-\\dfrac PA\\left(1+\\dfrac{6e_y}{h}+\\dfrac{6e_z}{b}\\right)$ at the worst corner; with the symmetric cases the condition for no tension is $\\dfrac{|e_y|}{h/6}+\\dfrac{|e_z|}{b/6}\\le1$: a rhombus (diamond) with half-diagonals h/6 and b/6.\n5. Meaning: inside the kern the whole section is in compression: crucial for masonry, unreinforced concrete and soil footings that cannot take tension (no separation), and for gasketed joints.\nCheck: at e = h/6 the stress at the far edge is zero and the near-edge stress is 2P/A. Probe: effect of a bolted flange.","answers":["σ = −(P/A)(1 ± ec/r_g²); no tension if e ≤ r_g²/c","Rectangle e ≤ h/6 (middle third); circle e ≤ R/4","Rectangle with eccentricity both ways: |e_y|/(h/6) + |e_z|/(b/6) ≤ 1 (rhombus)"],"src":"Hibbeler §8.2 Combined loadings (eccentric axial load, core of a section)"},{"id":"8.3","title":"Shaft under bending and torque","q":"A solid circular shaft of diameter d carries a bending moment M and a torque T at a section (neglect transverse shear and axial force). (a) Write the stresses at the most heavily loaded surface point (bending stress σ and torsional shear τ) in terms of M, T, d. (b) Using the Mohr\u0027s circle for this plane stress state, derive the principal stresses and the maximum in-plane shear stress. (c) Define an equivalent pure-bending moment M_e and an equivalent pure torque T_e that would give the same maximum normal stress and the same maximum shear stress respectively. (d) Why is neglecting transverse shear justified for a long shaft?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. $I=\\pi d^4/64$, $J=\\pi d^4/32$, c = d/2: $\\sigma=\\dfrac{Mc}{I}=\\dfrac{32M}{\\pi d^3}$, $\\tau=\\dfrac{Tc}{J}=\\dfrac{16T}{\\pi d^3}$ (the surface point of max bending also has max torsion shear).\n2. Mohr: centre σ/2, radius $R=\\sqrt{(\\sigma/2)^2+\\tau^2}=\\dfrac{16}{\\pi d^3}\\sqrt{M^2+T^2}$. $\\sigma_{1,2}=\\dfrac\\sigma2\\pm R=\\dfrac{16}{\\pi d^3}\\left(M\\pm\\sqrt{M^2+T^2}\\right)$; $\\tau_{max,in-plane}=R=\\dfrac{16}{\\pi d^3}\\sqrt{M^2+T^2}$.\n3. Equivalent bending moment: set $\\sigma_1=\\dfrac{32M_e}{\\pi d^3}\\Rightarrow M_e=\\dfrac12\\left(M+\\sqrt{M^2+T^2}\\right)$. Equivalent torque (same maximum shear $16T_e/\\pi d^3$): $T_e=\\sqrt{M^2+T^2}$.\n4. Limits: T = 0 gives M_e = M, T_e = M; M = 0 gives M_e = T/2, T_e = T (pure torsion: σ1 = τ).\n5. Transverse shear τ_V ≈ 4V/3A ~ M/(L A) while σ ~ M c/I: ratio ~ d/L, negligible for L ≫ d; it is also zero at the surface point where bending stress peaks.\nProbe: the Tresca design (absolute max shear) uses τ_abs = max(R, σ1/2); show which governs when σ1 \u003e 0, σ2 \u003c 0 (R governs).","answers":["σ = 32M/(πd³), τ = 16T/(πd³)","σ_{1,2} = (16/πd³)(M ± √(M²+T²)); τ_max = (16/πd³)√(M²+T²)","M_e = ½(M + √(M²+T²)); T_e = √(M²+T²); shear from V is smaller by about d/L"],"src":"Hibbeler §8.2 State of Stress Caused by Combined Loadings; §10.7 Theories of Failure"},{"id":"9.1","title":"Why torsion failures look different in chalk and steel","q":"A point in a circular shaft under torque is in a state of pure shear τ₀ (σ_x = σ_y = 0, τ_xy = τ₀). (a) Derive the normal and shear stress on a plane whose normal is at angle θ to the x axis, and hence the principal stresses and planes and the planes of maximum shear. (b) For a uniaxial tension σ₀, repeat the maximum shear. (c) Use these results to explain why a brittle material (such as chalk, weak in tension) breaks along a 45° helix in torsion, whereas a ductile mild-steel bar shears off across a plane perpendicular to its axis, and why a tensile specimen of the same steel shows slip lines at 45°.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Transformation with σ_x = σ_y = 0: $\\sigma_{x\u0027}=\\tau_0\\sin2\\theta$, $\\tau_{x\u0027y\u0027}=\\tau_0\\cos2\\theta$.\n2. Principal planes: τ_x\u0027y\u0027 = 0 at 2θ = 90° → θ = 45° (and 135°), where $\\sigma=\\pm\\tau_0$. So pure shear = tension τ₀ along one 45° line and compression τ₀ along the other. Maximum shear τ₀ at θ = 0° and 90° (the original x and y planes), 45° away from the principal planes, with σ = 0 on them.\n3. Uniaxial σ₀ (σ_x = σ₀, σ_y = 0, τ = 0): $\\tau_{max}=\\sigma_0/2$ at θ = 45°, with normal stress σ₀/2 on those planes.\n4. Torsion of a ductile material (weak in shear, strong in tension): yield/fracture is governed by the maximum shear, acting on the transverse plane (and the longitudinal plane): flat shear failure. Brittle (weak in tension): governed by maximum tensile stress τ₀ on the 45° helical plane: spiral fracture.\n5. Tension test of steel: slip lines at 45° because the maximum shear stress σ₀/2 acts there; yielding in shear when σ₀ = 2τ_Y (Tresca).\nProbe: where would a cast-iron compression specimen fail? (compression: shear failure at about 45°.) Why does the 45° line also appear on wood in torsion?","answers":["σ_x\u0027 = τ₀ sin2θ; τ_x\u0027y\u0027 = τ₀ cos2θ; principal stresses ±τ₀ at 45°; maximum shear τ₀ on θ = 0°, 90°","Uniaxial: τ_max = σ₀/2 at 45°","Ductile: shear failure on transverse plane; brittle: tension failure on 45° helix (σ₁ = τ₀)"],"src":"Hibbeler §9.3-9.4 Principal stresses, Mohr\u0027s circle (torsion failure of brittle vs ductile specimens)"},{"id":"9.2","title":"Absolute maximum shear stress: three circles","q":"A point is in plane stress with in-plane principal stresses σ₁ ≥ σ₂ and the out-of-plane stress zero (σ₃ = 0). (a) Draw (describe) the three Mohr\u0027s circles for the principal stresses and state the absolute maximum shear stress in terms of σ₁, σ₂ for the three cases: both tensile, opposite signs, both compressive. (b) Apply to the outer surface of a thin-walled cylinder (σ₁ = pr/t, σ₂ = pr/2t) and of a thin spherical shell (σ₁ = σ₂ = pr/2t): compare the in-plane maximum shear with the absolute maximum. (c) Explain why only the opposite-signs case has the in-plane maximum equal to the absolute one.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Principal stresses σ₁, σ₂, σ₃ = 0 order them as max ≥ int ≥ min. Three circles with diameters |σ₁−σ₂|, |σ₂−σ₃|, |σ₁−σ₃|. Absolute maximum shear = radius of the largest circle = (σ_max − σ_min)/2.\n2. Both tensile (σ₁ ≥ σ₂ \u003e 0): σ_max = σ₁, σ_min = 0: $\\tau_{abs}=\\sigma_1/2$. Opposite signs (σ₁ \u003e 0 \u003e σ₂): $\\tau_{abs}=(\\sigma_1-\\sigma_2)/2$ = in-plane maximum. Both compressive: $\\tau_{abs}=|\\sigma_2|/2$.\n3. Cylinder: in-plane max shear $(\\sigma_1-\\sigma_2)/2=\\dfrac{pr}{4t}$ but $\\tau_{abs}=\\dfrac{\\sigma_1}{2}=\\dfrac{pr}{2t}$, twice larger, acting on planes at 45° to the surface about the axial direction.\n4. Sphere: in-plane shear is zero (circle is a point) but $\\tau_{abs}=\\dfrac{\\sigma_1}{2}=\\dfrac{pr}{4t}$ on planes 45° to the surface.\n5. The in-plane circle spans σ₂ to σ₁; the third stress (zero) lies outside this interval unless σ₁ and σ₂ have opposite signs, in which case the in-plane circle is the biggest.\nProbe: on the inside surface σ₃ = −p; for r/t ≫ 1 the correction is small. Why does a ductile Tresca failure of a plane-stress cylinder depend on σ₁ alone? (since τ_abs = σ₁/2.)","answers":["τ_abs = (σ_max − σ_min)/2 with σ₃ = 0 included: σ₁/2 (both tensile), (σ₁−σ₂)/2 (opposite signs), |σ₂|/2 (both compressive)","Cylinder: in-plane pr/4t, absolute pr/2t; sphere: in-plane 0, absolute pr/4t","In-plane maximum equals the absolute maximum only if σ₁ and σ₂ have opposite signs"],"src":"Hibbeler §9.5 Absolute Maximum Shear Stress"},{"id":"9.3","title":"Pressurised cylinder with torque: which shear governs?","q":"A closed thin-walled cylinder of mean radius r and thickness t has internal pressure p and carries a torque T. At the outer surface the stress components are σ_a = pr/(2t) (axial), σ_h = pr/t (hoop), τ = T/(2πr²t). (a) Derive the principal stresses σ₁, σ₂ in terms of a = pr/(4t) and τ. (b) Find the torque T* at which σ₂ changes sign. (c) Give the absolute maximum shear stress for T \u003c T* and for T \u003e T*, and show it is continuous at T*.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Mohr\u0027s circle: centre $\\dfrac{\\sigma_a+\\sigma_h}{2}=\\dfrac{3pr}{4t}=3a$, radius $\\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\sigma_h-\\sigma_a}{2}\\right)^2+\\tau^2}=\\sqrt{a^2+\\tau^2}$ (half the difference pr/t − pr/2t = pr/4t = a). $\\sigma_{1,2}=3a\\pm\\sqrt{a^2+\\tau^2}$.\n2. σ₂ = 0 when $9a^2=a^2+\\tau^2\\Rightarrow\\tau=2\\sqrt2\\,a=\\dfrac{pr}{\\sqrt2\\,t}$ (also from σ₁σ₂ = σ_aσ_h − τ² = 0). With $T=2\\pi r^2t\\tau$: $T^*=\\sqrt2\\,\\pi pr^3$.\n3. For T \u003c T*: both σ₁, σ₂ \u003e 0 and σ₃ = 0 (outer surface), so $\\tau_{abs}=\\sigma_1/2=\\dfrac12\\left(3a+\\sqrt{a^2+\\tau^2}\\right)$. For T \u003e T*: σ₂ \u003c 0 \u003c σ₁, so $\\tau_{abs}=\\dfrac{\\sigma_1-\\sigma_2}{2}=\\sqrt{a^2+\\tau^2}$ (in-plane maximum).\n4. At T*: $\\sqrt{a^2+8a^2}=3a$ gives σ₁ = 6a, σ₂ = 0; $\\sigma_1/2=3a=\\sqrt{a^2+\\tau^2}$ ✓ continuous.\nChecks: T = 0 gives σ₁ = 4a = pr/t, σ₂ = 2a = pr/2t ✓, τ_abs = 2a = pr/2t. p = 0 (a = 0): σ = ±τ.\nProbe: using the in-plane maximum shear only underestimates τ_abs when T \u003c T* (e.g. at T = 0: a vs 2a).","answers":["σ_{1,2} = 3a ± √(a² + τ²), a = pr/4t, τ = T/(2πr²t)","σ₂ changes sign at τ = pr/(√2 t), i.e. T* = √2 π p r³","τ_abs = σ₁/2 = (3a + √(a²+τ²))/2 for T \u003c T*; τ_abs = √(a²+τ²) for T \u003e T*; continuous (3a) at T*"],"src":"Hibbeler §8.1-8.2 with §9.5 Absolute Maximum Shear Stress"},{"id":"10.1","title":"Reducing a 0°/60°/120° strain rosette","q":"Three electrical strain gauges are bonded at a point on a free surface at angles 0°, 60° and 120° from the x axis and read ε_a, ε_b, ε_c. (a) Starting from the transformation ε_θ = ε_x cos²θ + ε_y sin²θ + γ_xy sinθ cosθ, derive ε_x, ε_y, γ_xy in terms of the three readings. (b) Show that ε_a + ε_b + ε_c = (3/2)(ε_x + ε_y), and explain what this says about the strain invariant. (c) Explain why two gauges are not enough in general, and why the principal directions then follow from the three readings. (d) State what extra assumption you need to obtain stresses and what that implies for ε_z.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. θ = 0: $\\varepsilon_a=\\varepsilon_x$. θ = 60°: cos² = 1/4, sin² = 3/4, sin cos = √3/4: $\\varepsilon_b=\\tfrac14\\varepsilon_x+\\tfrac34\\varepsilon_y+\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}{4}\\gamma_{xy}$. θ = 120°: sin cos = −√3/4: $\\varepsilon_c=\\tfrac14\\varepsilon_x+\\tfrac34\\varepsilon_y-\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}{4}\\gamma_{xy}$.\n2. Add: $\\varepsilon_b+\\varepsilon_c=\\tfrac12\\varepsilon_x+\\tfrac32\\varepsilon_y\\Rightarrow\\varepsilon_y=\\dfrac{2(\\varepsilon_b+\\varepsilon_c)-\\varepsilon_a}{3}$. Subtract: $\\varepsilon_b-\\varepsilon_c=\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}{2}\\gamma_{xy}\\Rightarrow\\gamma_{xy}=\\dfrac{2(\\varepsilon_b-\\varepsilon_c)}{\\sqrt3}$. And ε_x = ε_a.\n3. Sum: $\\varepsilon_a+\\varepsilon_b+\\varepsilon_c=\\varepsilon_x+\\tfrac12\\varepsilon_x+\\tfrac32\\varepsilon_y=\\tfrac32(\\varepsilon_x+\\varepsilon_y)$: the sum is proportional to the first invariant ε_x + ε_y (independent of axes; three gauges 60° apart sample the same average).\n4. Three unknowns (ε_x, ε_y, γ_xy) need three independent readings; two gauges leave one unknown (the shear). Principal strains and directions: $\\tan2\\theta_p=\\dfrac{\\gamma_{xy}}{\\varepsilon_x-\\varepsilon_y}$, $\\varepsilon_{1,2}=\\dfrac{\\varepsilon_x+\\varepsilon_y}{2}\\pm\\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\varepsilon_x-\\varepsilon_y}{2}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\gamma_{xy}}{2}\\right)^2}$.\n5. Free surface: plane stress (σ_z = 0), so $\\varepsilon_z=-\\dfrac{\\nu}{1-\\nu}(\\varepsilon_x+\\varepsilon_y)\\ne0$.\nProbe: check ε_θ at a gauge identical readings: ε_a = ε_b = ε_c gives γ = 0, ε_x = ε_y (isotropic). Equal readings at 0/45/90 gives γ = 2ε_b − ε_a − ε_c.","answers":["ε_x = ε_a; ε_y = [2(ε_b + ε_c) − ε_a]/3; γ_xy = 2(ε_b − ε_c)/√3","ε_a + ε_b + ε_c = (3/2)(ε_x + ε_y): proportional to the strain invariant","Three gauges are needed for three unknowns; plane stress gives ε_z = −ν(ε_x+ε_y)/(1−ν)"],"src":"Hibbeler §10.5 Strain Rosettes"},{"id":"10.2","title":"Deriving G = E/(2(1+ν)) from pure shear","q":"An isotropic linear elastic material has Young\u0027s modulus E, Poisson\u0027s ratio ν, and shear modulus G defined by τ = Gγ. An element is loaded in pure shear τ. (a) Find the principal stresses and the corresponding principal strains using the generalized Hooke\u0027s law. (b) Use the strain transformation to find γ_xy from these principal strains (or equivalently the 45° normal strain) and thereby derive G in terms of E and ν. (c) Deduce the allowed range of ν from G \u003e 0 and discuss the limiting values G = E/3 and G = E/2.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Pure shear τ on x-y axes: principal stresses σ₁ = +τ, σ₂ = −τ at 45° (σ₃ = 0).\n2. Hooke: $\\varepsilon_1=\\dfrac{1}{E}(\\tau+\\nu\\tau)=\\dfrac{(1+\\nu)\\tau}{E}$, $\\varepsilon_2=-\\varepsilon_1$.\n3. Strain transformation: in the x-y axes, the principal strains lie at 45°, so $\\varepsilon_1=\\dfrac{\\gamma_{xy}}{2}$ (for ε_x = ε_y = 0 the Mohr circle of strain has radius γ/2). Hence $\\gamma_{xy}=\\dfrac{2(1+\\nu)\\tau}{E}$.\n4. Definition τ = Gγ gives $G=\\dfrac{E}{2(1+\\nu)}$.\n5. G \u003e 0 and E \u003e 0 require ν \u003e −1. Together with K \u003e 0 (ν \u003c 1/2) the permitted range is −1 \u003c ν \u003c 1/2. ν = 1/2 gives G = E/3 (incompressible); ν = 0 gives G = E/2 (no lateral contraction; E = 2G).\nProbe: for typical metals ν ≈ 0.3 gives G ≈ 0.385 E (about 38 per cent). What if the material is anisotropic (e.g. wood, composites)? Then E, G, ν are independent.","answers":["σ₁ = τ, σ₂ = −τ at 45°; ε₁ = (1+ν)τ/E","γ_xy = 2ε₁ = 2(1+ν)τ/E, so G = E/[2(1+ν)]","G \u003e 0 ⇒ ν \u003e −1; with K \u003e 0, −1 \u003c ν \u003c 1/2; G = E/3 at ν = 1/2, E/2 at ν = 0"],"src":"Hibbeler §10.6 Material-Property Relationships"},{"id":"10.3","title":"Same strains, different stresses: plane stress versus plane strain","q":"At a point the principal in-plane strains ε₁ and ε₂ are known (E, ν given). (a) Assuming plane stress (σ₃ = 0), invert Hooke\u0027s law to obtain σ₁, σ₂ in terms of ε₁, ε₂, and find ε₃. (b) Assuming instead plane strain (ε₃ = 0), find σ₁, σ₂ and the out-of-plane stress σ₃. (c) Which assumption gives the larger stresses for the same measured strains? Where in a structure does each model apply (thin plate surface versus long dam or pipe far from its ends)?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Plane stress: $\\varepsilon_1=(\\sigma_1-\\nu\\sigma_2)/E$, $\\varepsilon_2=(\\sigma_2-\\nu\\sigma_1)/E$. Solve: $\\sigma_1=\\dfrac{E(\\varepsilon_1+\\nu\\varepsilon_2)}{1-\\nu^2}$, $\\sigma_2=\\dfrac{E(\\varepsilon_2+\\nu\\varepsilon_1)}{1-\\nu^2}$, and $\\varepsilon_3=-\\dfrac{\\nu}{E}(\\sigma_1+\\sigma_2)=-\\dfrac{\\nu}{1-\\nu}(\\varepsilon_1+\\varepsilon_2)$.\n2. Plane strain: $\\varepsilon_3=0=\\dfrac1E[\\sigma_3-\\nu(\\sigma_1+\\sigma_2)]\\Rightarrow\\sigma_3=\\nu(\\sigma_1+\\sigma_2)$. Substitute into $\\varepsilon_1=\\tfrac1E[\\sigma_1-\\nu\\sigma_2-\\nu\\sigma_3]$ etc.: $\\sigma_1=\\dfrac{E[(1-\\nu)\\varepsilon_1+\\nu\\varepsilon_2]}{(1+\\nu)(1-2\\nu)}$ and σ₂ by symmetry.\n3. Compare the coefficient of ε₁: plane stress $\\dfrac{E}{1-\\nu^2}$ against plane strain $\\dfrac{E(1-\\nu)}{(1+\\nu)(1-2\\nu)}$; the ratio plane strain / plane stress is $\\dfrac{(1-\\nu)^2}{1-2\\nu}\\ge1$, so plane strain is stiffer (the constraint of ε₃ = 0 adds ν σ₃ to stiffness), tending to ∞ as ν → 1/2 (incompressible).\n4. Plane stress: thin plates and free surfaces (σ₃ ≈ 0 there). Plane strain: a long prismatic body with fixed ends (dam, tunnel, pipe far from its ends), mid-thickness of a thick plate.\nProbe: a strain gauge measures only strain; why must the stress state assumption come from the geometry? Check: ν = 0 gives both equal to Eε.","answers":["Plane stress: σ₁ = E(ε₁+νε₂)/(1−ν²), ε₃ = −ν(ε₁+ε₂)/(1−ν)","Plane strain: σ₁ = E[(1−ν)ε₁+νε₂]/[(1+ν)(1−2ν)], σ₃ = ν(σ₁+σ₂)","Plane strain gives larger stresses by (1−ν)²/(1−2ν) in the ε₁ coefficient; plane stress for thin plates, plane strain for long constrained bodies"],"src":"Hibbeler §10.1 Plane Strain; §10.6 generalized Hooke\u0027s law"},{"id":"11.1","title":"Shape of a fully stressed cantilever","q":"A cantilever of constant width b carries a load P at its free end (x measured from the free end). Its depth h(x) is to be chosen so that the extreme-fibre bending stress equals the allowable σ_a at every section (fully stressed beam). (a) Derive h(x) from bending and sketch it. (b) Show that this shape would give h → 0 at the tip and so fails in shear; with allowable shear stress τ_a (use τ_max = 3V/2A for a rectangle) find the minimum depth and the distance x₀ from the tip at which shear stops governing. (c) Why is a fully stressed beam not necessarily the best design with respect to deflection and fabrication?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. M = Px. $\\sigma=\\dfrac{Mc}{I}=\\dfrac{Px(h/2)}{bh^3/12}=\\dfrac{6Px}{bh^2}=\\sigma_a\\Rightarrow h(x)=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{6Px}{b\\sigma_a}}$ (a parabola with vertex at the tip, axis along the beam).\n2. At the tip h → 0 but V = P still acts: $\\tau=\\dfrac{3P}{2bh}\\le\\tau_a\\Rightarrow h\\ge h_{min}=\\dfrac{3P}{2b\\tau_a}$ (V = P is constant along the beam, so this is the shear limit everywhere).\n3. Transition where bending depth equals h_min: $\\dfrac{6Px_0}{b\\sigma_a}=\\dfrac{9P^2}{4b^2\\tau_a^2}\\Rightarrow x_0=\\dfrac{3P\\sigma_a}{8b\\tau_a^2}$. For x \u003c x₀ use constant depth h_min (shear governs), for x \u003e x₀ use the parabola.\n4. Check dimensions: Pσ_a/(bτ_a²) has dimensions of force/(length·stress) = length ✓.\n5. Fully stressed means uniform stress along the extreme fibres, minimum material for strength; deflection is not minimised and the curved profile is expensive to machine (often replaced by a stepped or linear taper). In a leaf spring (constant depth h, varying width b ∝ x) the same condition gives a triangular plan.\nProbe: the deflection of the parabola-depth beam is larger than the prismatic beam of the same root section at same load. Why? (Smaller I along the length.)","answers":["h(x) = √(6Px/(bσ_a)) (parabolic), from σ = 6Px/(bh²) = σ_a","Shear requires h ≥ 3P/(2bτ_a)","Shear governs for x \u003c x₀ = 3Pσ_a/(8bτ_a²)"],"src":"Hibbeler §11.3 Fully Stressed Beams"},{"id":"11.2","title":"Strongest versus stiffest beam from a round log","q":"A rectangular beam of width b and depth h is to be cut from a circular log of diameter d (so b² + h² = d²) and loaded with the bending axis horizontal. (a) Find the ratio h/b that gives maximum bending strength (maximum section modulus S = bh²/6). (b) Find the ratio h/b that gives maximum stiffness (maximum I = bh³/12). (c) Explain why they differ and which criterion applies to a floor joist where deflection limits govern, and which to a short beam with a heavy load.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Strength: $S=\\dfrac{b(d^2-b^2)}{6}$. $\\dfrac{dS}{db}=\\dfrac{d^2-3b^2}{6}=0\\Rightarrow b=\\dfrac{d}{\\sqrt3}$, $h=\\sqrt{\\dfrac23}\\,d$, so $h/b=\\sqrt2$.\n2. Stiffness: $I=\\dfrac{b(d^2-b^2)^{3/2}}{12}$. $\\dfrac{dI}{db}\\propto(d^2-b^2)^{3/2}-3b^2(d^2-b^2)^{1/2}=0\\Rightarrow d^2-b^2=3b^2\\Rightarrow b=\\dfrac d2$, $h=\\dfrac{\\sqrt3}{2}d$, $h/b=\\sqrt3$.\n3. Both are maxima (S and I vanish at b = 0 and b = d). The stiffest section is deeper (√3 \u003e √2) because I grows as h³ while S grows as h²: a deep section is rewarded more when stiffness is the criterion.\n4. Joists limited by deflection (L/360): use the stiffer ratio √3 (I governs). Short, heavily loaded beams limited by σ_max: use √2 (S governs).\nChecks: area at the strength optimum, bh = √2 d²/3 ≈ 0.471d²; at stiffness optimum bh = √3 d²/4 ≈ 0.433d². Probe: lateral stability (lateral-torsional buckling) limits how deep a narrow section can be.","answers":["Strongest: b = d/√3, h = √(2/3) d, h/b = √2","Stiffest: b = d/2, h = (√3/2) d, h/b = √3","Different because S ∝ bh² while I ∝ bh³; deflection-limited joists use √3, strength-limited short beams √2"],"src":"Hibbeler §11.1-11.2 Beam design and section modulus (beam cut from a log)"},{"id":"11.3","title":"Shaft design for transmitted power","q":"A solid circular shaft must transmit power P at rotation frequency f (revolutions per second) with allowable shear stress τ_a. (a) Derive the minimum diameter d. (b) How does d change if the same power is transmitted at doubled speed? (c) A hollow shaft with inner/outer radius ratio η = c_i/c_o must transmit the same torque at the same τ_a. Derive the ratio of the cross-sectional area (mass) of the hollow shaft to that of the solid one in terms of η, and discuss the limits η → 0 and η → 1 (including why thin walls are not used without limit).","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Power P = Tω with ω = 2πf: $T=\\dfrac{P}{2\\pi f}$. Torsion: $\\tau_a=\\dfrac{16T}{\\pi d^3}\\Rightarrow d=\\left(\\dfrac{16T}{\\pi\\tau_a}\\right)^{1/3}=\\left(\\dfrac{8P}{\\pi^2f\\tau_a}\\right)^{1/3}$.\n2. Doubling f at fixed P halves T, so $d\\to2^{-1/3}d\\approx0.794\\,d$ (area falls to $2^{-2/3}\\approx0.63$): high-speed shafts are lighter.\n3. Hollow: $T=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\tau_ac_o^3(1-\\eta^4)$. Solid of radius c: $T=\\dfrac\\pi2\\tau_ac^3$. Equal T: $c_o^3(1-\\eta^4)=c^3$, i.e. $c_o^2=c^2(1-\\eta^4)^{-2/3}$. Area ratio $\\dfrac{A_h}{A_s}=\\dfrac{\\pi c_o^2(1-\\eta^2)}{\\pi c^2}=\\dfrac{1-\\eta^2}{(1-\\eta^4)^{2/3}}$.\n4. η → 0: ratio → 1. For η = 1/2: ≈ 0.78. η → 1: ratio $\\approx\\dfrac{(1-\\eta^2)^{1/3}}{2^{2/3}}\\to0$ in principle (thin wall; material at the large radius is used efficiently since τ ∝ ρ).\n5. In practice thin walls buckle (wrinkling), are difficult to make, are sensitive to dents and stress concentration at keyways; and the outer radius grows, so bearings and couplings are bigger.\nProbe: the polar moment of inertia per unit mass grows as r² for a thin ring; compare with solid, J/A = c²/2 versus c_o²(1+η²)/2.","answers":["d = (16T/(πτ_a))^{1/3} = (8P/(π² f τ_a))^{1/3}, T = P/(2πf)","Doubling f at the same P: d reduces by 2^{−1/3} ≈ 0.79","A_hollow/A_solid = (1−η²)/(1−η⁴)^{2/3} \u003c 1 and → 0 as η → 1 (limited by wall buckling)"],"src":"Hibbeler §5.3 Power Transmission; §11.4 Shaft Design"},{"id":"12.1","title":"Propped cantilever on an elastic prop","q":"A uniform cantilever of length L and flexural rigidity EI, loaded by a uniform load w over its whole length, is supported at its free end B by a linear spring of stiffness k (force per unit deflection). (a) Treat the spring reaction R as the redundant and use superposition and compatibility to find R. (b) Give the fixed-end moment M_A. (c) Check the limits k → ∞ (rigid prop) and k → 0, and write the non-dimensional stiffness ratio that controls the answer.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Release the spring. Deflection at B from w: $\\Delta_w=\\dfrac{wL^4}{8EI}$ (down). From the spring force R (up): $\\Delta_R=\\dfrac{RL^3}{3EI}$ (up).\n2. Compatibility: the net deflection of B equals the spring compression R/k: $\\dfrac{wL^4}{8EI}-\\dfrac{RL^3}{3EI}=\\dfrac Rk$.\n3. Solve: $R=\\dfrac{wL^4/8EI}{L^3/3EI+1/k}=\\dfrac{3wL}{8}\\cdot\\dfrac{1}{1+3EI/(kL^3)}$.\n4. Equilibrium of the fixed end: $M_A=\\dfrac{wL^2}{2}-RL$ (hogging).\n5. Limits: k → ∞: R = 3wL/8 and M_A = wL²/8 (standard propped cantilever). k → 0: R → 0 and M_A = wL²/2 (plain cantilever). The controlling parameter is $\\kappa=\\dfrac{3EI}{kL^3}$, the ratio of the beam\u0027s own tip flexibility to the spring\u0027s; R and M_A vary monotonically with κ.\nProbe: R/k is the spring deflection, hence the tip deflection $\\dfrac{wL^4}{8EI}\\dfrac{1}{1+\\dfrac{kL^3}{3EI}}$. A statically indeterminate reaction depends on stiffness (not only on geometry) when a support is flexible.","answers":["R = (3wL/8)/(1 + 3EI/(kL³))","M_A = wL²/2 − RL","k → ∞: R = 3wL/8, M_A = wL²/8; k → 0: R = 0, M_A = wL²/2"],"src":"Hibbeler §12.9 Statically indeterminate beams, method of superposition (spring support)"},{"id":"12.2","title":"Building beam deflections from one point-load result","q":"A cantilever of length L and flexural rigidity EI carries a transverse load P at distance a from the fixed end (0 \u003c a \u003c L). (a) Using the deflection and slope of a cantilever with an end load, derive the deflection of the free end. (b) Use this result as an influence (Green\u0027s) function to obtain the free-end deflection when the beam carries a uniform load w over its whole length, and over only the first length a from the fixed end. (c) State what the form of the result tells you about which loads matter most for tip deflection.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. For a load P at x = a the part between a and L is straight with slope equal to that at a: $\\theta_a=\\dfrac{Pa^2}{2EI}$, $v_a=\\dfrac{Pa^3}{3EI}$. Tip: $v(L)=v_a+\\theta_a(L-a)=\\dfrac{Pa^3}{3EI}+\\dfrac{Pa^2(L-a)}{2EI}=\\dfrac{Pa^2(3L-a)}{6EI}$.\n2. Linear elasticity allows superposition: replace P by $w\\,da\u0027$ at position a\u0027 and integrate. Full length: $v(L)=\\dfrac{w}{6EI}\\int_0^La\u0027^2(3L-a\u0027)\\,da\u0027=\\dfrac{w}{6EI}\\left(L^4-\\dfrac{L^4}{4}\\right)=\\dfrac{wL^4}{8EI}$ ✓ (standard).\n3. Load over only 0 ≤ x ≤ a: $v(L)=\\dfrac{w}{6EI}\\left(La^3-\\dfrac{a^4}{4}\\right)=\\dfrac{wa^3(4L-a)}{24EI}$. Check a = L: wL⁴/(8EI) ✓.\n4. The kernel $a^2(3L-a)$ grows like a² at small a: loads near the fixed end contribute very little; most of the tip deflection comes from loads near the free end (large lever arm and long beam length to rotate). Maxwell\u0027s reciprocity says the same function gives the deflection at a due to a unit load at the tip: $v(a)=\\dfrac{Pa^2(3L-a)}{6EI}$.\nProbe: units check [P a² L/(EI)] = length ✓; a → L gives PL³/3EI ✓.","answers":["Tip deflection: Pa²(3L − a)/(6EI)","Uniform w over the full length: wL⁴/(8EI); over 0 ≤ x ≤ a: wa³(4L − a)/(24EI)","Loads near the free end dominate; reciprocity gives the same function for the deflection at x = a from a tip load"],"src":"Hibbeler §12.5 Method of Superposition"},{"id":"12.3","title":"Moment-area theorems: where they come from and how to use them","q":"The moment-area method finds slopes and deflections of a beam from the area under its M/EI diagram. (a) Starting from the elastic curve d²v/dx² = M/EI, derive the two moment-area theorems: the change in slope between points A and B, and the vertical deviation t_B/A of the tangent at A from the elastic curve at B. (b) A cantilever of length L and constant EI carries a point load P at its free end. Use the theorems to find the tip slope and tip deflection, and explain why the fixed end is the natural reference tangent. (c) For a simply supported beam of span L with a central load P, explain why the method needs an extra geometric step to find the midspan deflection, and outline it.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Write $d\\theta=\\dfrac{M}{EI}dx$. Integrating from A to B: **Theorem 1** $\\theta_{B/A}=\\int_A^B\\dfrac{M}{EI}dx$ = area of the M/EI diagram between A and B.\n2. The tangents at the ends of an element $dx$ intercept a length $dt=x\\,d\\theta$ on a vertical line through B ($x$ measured from B). Integrating: **Theorem 2** $t_{B/A}=\\int_A^B x\\dfrac{M}{EI}dx=\\bar x\\,A_{M/EI}$, the first moment of the M/EI area about B. Note $t_{B/A}\\ne t_{A/B}$ in general.\n3. Cantilever: the tangent at the fixed end A is horizontal, so slopes and deviations from it ARE the actual slope and deflection. M/EI is a triangle of height $-PL/EI$ at A: area $-PL^2/(2EI)$, centroid $2L/3$ from B. So $\\theta_B=PL^2/(2EI)$ and $v_B=t_{B/A}=\\dfrac{PL^2}{2EI}\\cdot\\dfrac{2L}{3}=\\dfrac{PL^3}{3EI}$ (downward).\n4. Simply supported: no tangent is known to be horizontal in advance (except by symmetry at midspan; in general unknown). Find the end slope from $t_{B/A}$: $\\theta_A=t_{B/A}/L$; then deflection at C is $v_C=\\theta_A\\,(L/2)-t_{C/A}$. With symmetry: tangent at C is horizontal, so $v_C=t_{A/C}=\\dfrac12\\cdot\\dfrac L2\\cdot\\dfrac{PL}{4EI}\\cdot\\dfrac{2}{3}\\dfrac L2=\\dfrac{PL^3}{48EI}$.\nProbe: sign convention (positive M/EI area means counterclockwise rotation from A to B; positive t means B above the tangent at A); why small slopes are assumed ($\\tan\\theta\\approx\\theta$).","answers":["θ_B/A = area of the M/EI diagram from A to B; t_B/A = first moment of that area about B","Cantilever with tip load: θ_B = PL²/(2EI), v_B = PL³/(3EI); the fixed-end tangent is horizontal, so deviations are actual deflections","Simply supported, central P: use θ_A = t_B/A / L (or symmetry), then v_C = PL³/(48EI)"],"src":"Hibbeler §12.4 Slope and Displacement by the Moment-Area Method"},{"id":"13.1","title":"Critical loads for fixed-free and fixed-pinned columns","q":"A slender column of length L and constant flexural rigidity EI has an axial compressive load P. (a) For the column fixed at the base and free at the top, write the differential equation for the buckled shape using the top deflection δ, solve it and find P_cr and the effective-length factor K. (b) For the column fixed at the base and pinned at the top (a horizontal reaction R acts at the pin), derive the characteristic equation for the buckling load and give its smallest root and P_cr in the form π²EI/(KL)². (c) Why is the Euler load independent of the material strength, and what physical failure does it describe?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Fixed-free, x from the base, deflection v(x), top deflection δ: bending moment at x is $P(\\delta-v)$, so $EIv\u0027\u0027=P(\\delta-v)$, i.e. $v\u0027\u0027+k^2v=k^2\\delta$ with $k^2=P/EI$. With v(0) = 0, v\u0027(0) = 0: $v=\\delta(1-\\cos kx)$. Condition v(L) = δ gives δ cos kL = 0, δ ≠ 0 so $kL=\\dfrac\\pi2$: $P_{cr}=\\dfrac{\\pi^2EI}{4L^2}=\\dfrac{\\pi^2EI}{(2L)^2}$, K = 2.\n2. Fixed-pinned: moment at x is $-Pv+R(L-x)$ (from the top free body, R horizontal reaction at the pin). $EIv\u0027\u0027+Pv=R(L-x)$; particular solution $\\dfrac RP(L-x)$; general: $v=A\\cos kx+B\\sin kx+\\dfrac RP(L-x)$.\n3. BCs: v(0) = 0: A = −RL/P. v\u0027(0) = 0: Bk − R/P = 0, B = R/(Pk). v(L) = 0: $-\\dfrac{RL}{P}\\cos kL+\\dfrac{R}{Pk}\\sin kL=0\\Rightarrow\\tan kL=kL$.\n4. Smallest nonzero root kL ≈ 4.4934, so $P_{cr}=\\dfrac{(4.4934)^2EI}{L^2}\\approx\\dfrac{20.19EI}{L^2}=\\dfrac{\\pi^2EI}{(0.699L)^2}$, K ≈ 0.7.\n5. P_cr depends on EI/L², not on yield stress: instability is an elastic geometric bifurcation (the straight state loses stability), valid while the stress P_cr/A stays below the proportional limit. Failure involves large lateral deflection at stress far below yield for slender columns.\nProbe: pinned-pinned K = 1, fixed-fixed K = 0.5. Check the order K: 0.5 \u003c 0.7 \u003c 1 \u003c 2 (stiffer end conditions raise P_cr).","answers":["Fixed-free: v = δ(1 − cos kx), cos kL = 0, P_cr = π²EI/(4L²), K = 2","Fixed-pinned: tan kL = kL, kL ≈ 4.493, P_cr ≈ 20.19EI/L² = π²EI/(0.699L)²","Euler load depends on EI and L only: elastic bifurcation, not material strength"],"src":"Hibbeler §13.3 Columns Having Various Types of Supports"},{"id":"13.2","title":"Eccentric column: secant formula and its limit","q":"A pinned-pinned column of length L, flexural rigidity EI, area A, radius of gyration r and extreme-fibre distance c carries a compressive load P applied at eccentricity e at both ends. (a) Derive the maximum deflection v_max and the maximum moment. (b) Obtain the secant formula σ_max = (P/A)[1 + (ec/r²) sec((L/2r)√(P/EA))]. (c) Show what happens to v_max as P → P_cr (Euler) and what this means for the load that causes the stress σ_max to reach σ_Y. (d) Why does this formula remove the sharp distinction between a straight and a slightly eccentric column?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. The moment at section x: $M=-P(e+v)$ (e added to the lateral deflection v, measured from the line of the ends). $EIv\u0027\u0027=-P(v+e)\\Rightarrow v\u0027\u0027+k^2v=-k^2e$, $k^2=P/EI$.\n2. Solution with v(0) = v(L) = 0: $v=e\\left[\\tan\\dfrac{kL}{2}\\sin kx+\\cos kx-1\\right]$. At mid span: $v_{max}=e\\left[\\sec\\dfrac{kL}{2}-1\\right]$ (using $\\sin^2+\\cos^2=1$).\n3. Maximum moment: $M_{max}=P(e+v_{max})=Pe\\sec\\dfrac{kL}{2}$. Stress: $\\sigma_{max}=\\dfrac PA+\\dfrac{M_{max}c}{I}=\\dfrac PA\\left[1+\\dfrac{ec}{r^2}\\sec\\left(\\dfrac L{2r}\\sqrt{\\dfrac P{EA}}\\right)\\right]$ since $kL/2=\\dfrac L{2r}\\sqrt{P/EA}$.\n4. P → P_cr: kL/2 → π/2, sec → ∞, so v_max → ∞ for any e \u003e 0 (as e → 0, the amplitude is indeterminate, consistent with bifurcation). The failure load is where σ_max = σ_Y, always below P_cr and below σ_Y A; the load–deflection curve is nonlinear (deflection grows faster than P).\n5. The secant formula is not linear in P and cannot be solved for P in closed form (iterate). For e = 0 it reduces to the axial stress P/A, with buckling only when the straight form is perturbed. A real column always has an imperfection or load eccentricity, hence it deflects from the start and its strength tends to Euler\u0027s load for slender columns and to σ_Y A for short ones.\nChecks: kL/2 → 0 gives v_max → 0 ✓; small P: v_max ≈ e(kL)²/8 = PL²e/(8EI).","answers":["v_max = e[sec(kL/2) − 1], M_max = Pe sec(kL/2), k² = P/EI","σ_max = (P/A)[1 + (ec/r²) sec((L/2r)√(P/EA))]","As P → P_cr, v_max → ∞; the failure load lies below P_cr and is found where σ_max = σ_Y (iteratively)"],"src":"Hibbeler §13.4 The Secant Formula"},{"id":"13.3","title":"Inelastic buckling and the tangent modulus","q":"A column is made of a material that is linear elastic up to the proportional limit σ_pl and has a tangent modulus E_t(σ) \u003c E beyond it. (a) State the Euler critical stress in the elastic range and find the slenderness ratio (L/r)_c below which Euler\u0027s formula no longer applies. (b) Explain the tangent-modulus (Engesser) formula σ_cr = π²E_t/(KL/r)² for inelastic buckling and why E_t rather than E appears. (c) For a perfectly plastic material (E_t → 0 at σ_Y) what does the formula predict and what is the actual limit? (d) Describe how the reduced-modulus (double-modulus) and Shanley\u0027s analysis relate to the tangent-modulus load.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Elastic: $\\sigma_{cr}=\\dfrac{P_{cr}}{A}=\\dfrac{\\pi^2E}{(KL/r)^2}$. Euler valid while σ_cr ≤ σ_pl: $\\left(\\dfrac{KL}{r}\\right)\\ge\\left(\\dfrac{KL}{r}\\right)_c=\\pi\\sqrt{\\dfrac{E}{\\sigma_{pl}}}$.\n2. Beyond σ_pl the stress–strain curve is not linear. At the onset of buckling the infinitesimal change in strain over the section follows the tangent modulus $E_t=d\\sigma/d\\varepsilon$ (Engesser assumption: the column buckles at constant P, no strain reversal). Replacing E by E_t in the derivation of EIv\u0027\u0027 = M gives $P_{cr}=\\dfrac{\\pi^2E_tI}{(KL)^2}$, $\\sigma_{cr}=\\dfrac{\\pi^2E_t}{(KL/r)^2}$. Because E_t \u003c E, the inelastic critical load is lower than the Euler value.\n3. Perfectly plastic: E_t → 0 at σ_Y gives σ_cr → 0 in the formula, but the real column cannot support less than the squash load for short length, so the practical bound is σ_cr ≤ σ_Y (a short column crushes, not buckles). The formula is implemented iteratively in σ: E_t depends on σ, so solve σ = π²E_t(σ)/(KL/r)².\n4. Reduced (double) modulus: accounts for unloading on the convex side (modulus E) and loading on the concave side (E_t) so E_r lies between E_t and E, giving the upper bound. Shanley showed buckling begins at the tangent-modulus load, and that the column can carry a slightly higher load with increasing bending before reaching the reduced-modulus value: so P_t is the (conservative) design load.\nProbe: why is (L/r)_c typically ~100 for structural steel? (E ≈ 200 GPa, σ_pl ≈ 200 MPa gives π√1000 ≈ 99.)","answers":["σ_cr = π²E/(KL/r)², Euler valid for KL/r ≥ π√(E/σ_pl)","Inelastic: σ_cr = π²E_t/(KL/r)², E_t = dσ/dε at σ_cr, lower than elastic","Tangent modulus is the conservative lower bound; reduced modulus is the upper bound (Shanley); crushing limits σ_cr ≤ σ_Y"],"src":"Hibbeler §13.5 Inelastic Buckling"},{"id":"14.1","title":"Cantilever tip deflection by Castigliano, with shear","q":"A cantilever of length L, constant flexural rigidity EI and rectangular section (depth h, shear modulus G, area A) carries a tip load P and a uniform load w over its whole length. (a) Use Castigliano\u0027s second theorem to find the vertical tip deflection due to bending. (b) Compare the contributions of P and w for the same total load wL = P. (c) Estimate the shear-deflection contribution from the shear strain energy (form factor f_s = 6/5 for a rectangle) due to P only, and show its ratio to the bending part for P is of order (h/L)². When can shear deflection be ignored?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Take x from the free end: $M=-Px-\\dfrac{wx^2}{2}$, $\\dfrac{\\partial M}{\\partial P}=-x$. $\\Delta=\\dfrac{1}{EI}\\int_0^L\\left(Px+\\dfrac{wx^2}{2}\\right)x\\,dx=\\dfrac{PL^3}{3EI}+\\dfrac{wL^4}{8EI}$.\n2. Ratio (w term to P term): $\\dfrac{wL^4/8}{PL^3/3}=\\dfrac{3wL}{8P}$; for wL = P the uniform load contributes only 3/8 as much as the same load at the tip (it is distributed nearer the support).\n3. Shear: $U_s=\\int\\dfrac{f_sV^2}{2GA}dx$, V = P: $\\Delta_s=\\dfrac{\\partial U_s}{\\partial P}=\\dfrac{f_sPL}{GA}=\\dfrac{6PL}{5GA}$.\n4. Ratio: $\\dfrac{\\Delta_s}{\\Delta_b}=\\dfrac{6PL/5GA}{PL^3/3EI}=\\dfrac{18}{5}\\dfrac{E}{G}\\dfrac{I}{AL^2}$; with I/A = h²/12 and E/G = 2(1+ν): $\\dfrac{3}{5}(1+\\nu)\\left(\\dfrac hL\\right)^2$. For ν = 0.3 this is $0.78(h/L)^2$: below 1 per cent for L/h = 10 and negligible for L/h \u003e 10.\n5. Shear deflection becomes comparable for short deep beams (L/h ≲ 3), sandwich beams (soft core, low G) and timber.\nChecks: dimensions ✓; w = 0, h → 0 reduces to PL³/3EI. Probe: Castigliano requires a force at the point and direction of the deflection sought: for a point with no load use a dummy load P₀ and set it to zero.","answers":["Δ = PL³/(3EI) + wL⁴/(8EI)","For wL = P the uniform load gives 3/8 of the tip-load deflection","Δ_shear/Δ_bending ≈ (3/5)(1+ν)(h/L)²; negligible for slender beams (L/h \u003e 10)"],"src":"Hibbeler §14.2 Elastic Strain Energy (shear); §14.10 Castigliano\u0027s Theorem Applied to Beams"},{"id":"14.2","title":"Impact factor from energy conservation","q":"A weight W is dropped from a height h onto the end of a linear elastic structure (spring, bar or beam) whose static stiffness at the impact point is k (so the static deflection under W is Δ_st = W/k). (a) Using energy conservation, derive the maximum dynamic deflection Δ_max and the impact factor n = Δ_max/Δ_st. (b) Evaluate n for h = 0 (suddenly applied load) and for h ≫ Δ_st. (c) List the assumptions behind the formula and state which way each one errs. (d) What does this say about using a more flexible member to absorb impact (for a given maximum stress)?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Energy: loss of potential energy W(h + Δ_max) = elastic energy ½kΔ_max² (all energy stored, none dissipated). With k = W/Δ_st: $\\Delta_{max}^2-2\\Delta_{st}\\Delta_{max}-2h\\Delta_{st}=0$.\n2. $\\Delta_{max}=\\Delta_{st}\\left[1+\\sqrt{1+\\dfrac{2h}{\\Delta_{st}}}\\right]$, so $n=1+\\sqrt{1+\\dfrac{2h}{\\Delta_{st}}}$; stresses scale equally for a linear structure, $\\sigma_{max}=n\\sigma_{st}$.\n3. h = 0: n = 2 (suddenly applied load doubles deflection and stress). h ≫ Δ_st: $n\\approx\\sqrt{2h/\\Delta_{st}}$.\n4. Assumptions: (i) no energy lost in the contact or as heat/sound: overestimates n; (ii) the struck member has negligible mass (in reality part of the energy goes into its kinetic energy and the plastic/impact losses at contact are neglected): the simple formula normally overestimates the response, so it is conservative; (iii) material linear elastic, no plastic yielding; (iv) the weight stays in contact; (v) stress waves are ignored (valid when impact is slow relative to the wave transit time).\n5. For a given W and h, a larger Δ_st (lower stiffness) gives a smaller n and, for large h, $\\sigma_{max}\\approx\\sigma_{st}\\sqrt{2h/\\Delta_{st}}$: softer members have smaller dynamic stress (hence reduced-shank bolts, springs, and bumpers). But the deflection Δ_max ≈ √(2hΔ_st) increases, so there is a clearance trade-off.\nProbe: in terms of the energy: required energy capacity = W(h + Δ_max), so the member must store this at stress below σ_Y; strain energy per volume = σ²/2E.","answers":["Δ_max = Δ_st[1 + √(1 + 2h/Δ_st)], n = 1 + √(1 + 2h/Δ_st)","n = 2 for a suddenly applied load (h = 0); n ≈ √(2h/Δ_st) for large h","Larger Δ_st (flexible member) reduces n and the dynamic stress; formula assumes no losses and negligible mass, so it is conservative"],"src":"Hibbeler §14.4 Impact Loading"},{"id":"14.3","title":"Quarter-circular bar: deflection with a dummy load","q":"A thin quarter-circular bar of radius R (flexural rigidity EI constant) is fixed at A and free at B. The centre of curvature is O; A lies on the horizontal line through O at distance R, and B lies on the vertical line through O at distance R above it. A downward vertical load P acts at B. Neglect axial and shear energy. (a) Using Castigliano\u0027s theorem find the vertical deflection of B. (b) Introduce a dummy horizontal force Q at B to find the horizontal deflection of B and its direction. (c) Explain why the dummy-load method is needed, and why neglecting axial and shear energy is justified for R ≫ cross-section depth.","figs":[],"figDesc":"Quarter-circle bar, centre O, radius R; fixed end A at distance R from O on the horizontal line; free end B at distance R above O on the vertical line; downward load P at B.","sol":"1. Let φ be measured at O from the vertical line OB to the section: a section is at $(R\\sin\\varphi,\\ R\\cos\\varphi)$ relative to O, 0 ≤ φ ≤ π/2 (φ = π/2 at A), $ds=R\\,d\\varphi$. Lever arm of P (vertical) about the section: horizontal distance $R\\sin\\varphi$.\n2. $M=PR\\sin\\varphi$, $\\dfrac{\\partial M}{\\partial P}=R\\sin\\varphi$. $\\Delta_V=\\dfrac1{EI}\\int M\\dfrac{\\partial M}{\\partial P}ds=\\dfrac{PR^3}{EI}\\int_0^{\\pi/2}\\sin^2\\varphi\\,d\\varphi=\\dfrac{\\pi PR^3}{4EI}$ (downward).\n3. Dummy horizontal force Q at B (positive to the right): the lever arm of Q about the section is the vertical distance $R(1-\\cos\\varphi)$ and its moment has the opposite sense to that of P about the section. $M=PR\\sin\\varphi-QR(1-\\cos\\varphi)$, $\\dfrac{\\partial M}{\\partial Q}\\Big|_{Q=0}=-R(1-\\cos\\varphi)$.\n4. $\\Delta_H=\\dfrac{1}{EI}\\int_0^{\\pi/2}PR\\sin\\varphi\\,[-R(1-\\cos\\varphi)]R\\,d\\varphi=-\\dfrac{PR^3}{EI}\\left[1-\\dfrac12\\right]=-\\dfrac{PR^3}{2EI}$. The negative sign means B moves opposite to the assumed Q, i.e. to the left (away from A), by $\\dfrac{PR^3}{2EI}$.\n5. A dummy force is needed because Castigliano\u0027s derivative is with respect to a load at the point and in the direction of the desired deflection; P is vertical, so the horizontal deflection needs Q (set to 0 after differentiating).\n6. Axial and shear energies are of order $(t/R)^2$ times the bending energy, negligible for slender bars.\nProbe: check the dimensions PR³/EI = length ✓; the vertical deflection exceeds the horizontal by π/2.","answers":["Vertical deflection of B = πPR³/(4EI) downward","Horizontal deflection of B = PR³/(2EI), directed away from A (dummy Q gives −PR³/2EI)","Dummy force is used because the derivative must be taken w.r.t. a force at the point and in the direction of the deflection; axial and shear energy are negligible for R ≫ depth"],"src":"Hibbeler §14.10 Castigliano\u0027s Theorem Applied to Beams (curved bar, dummy load)"}];
